WEBVTT

00:00:00.490 --> 00:00:08.930
<v Ira Glass>During Bill Jerke's years as a state legislator, the Colorado Union of Taxpayers rated him as one of the biggest enemies of taxes in the state.
<v Ira Glass>比尔·杰克担任州议员期间，科罗拉多州纳税人联盟将其评为该州最大的反税收斗士之一。

00:00:08.930 --> 00:00:15.930
<v Bill Jerke>I think three years out of eight, I was either the outright winner or tied for most conservative legislator.
<v Bill Jerke>在八年的任期里，有三年我认为自己要么是绝对的赢家，要么并列为最保守的议员。

00:00:15.930 --> 00:00:20.040
<v Ira Glass>He helped organize the Tea Party chapter in Greeley. And he was proud to let me know--
<v Ira Glass>他协助组织了格里利的分支茶党。而且他大方地让我知道——

00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:23.980
<v Bill Jerke>I'm a big supporter of Juan Williams. Go on.
<v Bill Jerke>我是胡安·威廉姆斯的坚定支持者。请继续。

00:00:23.980 --> 00:00:33.000
<v Ira Glass>The politics this year are so strange that this anti-tax Republican has found himself spending the fall stumping the state, doing something he's never done.
<v Ira Glass>今年的政治局势如此诡异，以至于这位反税收的共和党人发现自己在这个秋天走遍全州进行拉票，做了一件他从未做过的事。

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:39.320
<v Ira Glass>Did you ever picture that you'd be somebody going out, talking to Republican groups, arguing against tax cuts?
<v Ira Glass>你曾想过自己会走出去，跟共和党团体对话，反对减税吗?

00:00:39.320 --> 00:00:45.390
<v Bill Jerke>It is hard to picture, frankly, that that's a position I would find myself in.
<v Bill Jerke>坦白说，很难想象我会发现自己处于这种立场。

00:00:45.390 --> 00:01:11.870
<v Ira Glass>Jerke is trying to defeat three ballot initiatives that come before Colorado voters next week. Initiatives 60, 61, and 101 together would strangle most of the ways a government brings in money. They'd cut Colorado's personal income taxes by a fourth, limit the ability of state and local governments to issue bonds, and force the state government to take over $2 billion in education funding that's now covered by local governments, but without the means to pay for that new cost.
<v Ira Glass>杰克正试图击败下周科罗拉多州选民将要面对的三项投票动议。第60、61和101号动议联合起来，将扼杀政府筹集资金的大部分途径。它们将把科罗拉多州的个人所得税削减四分之一，限制州和地方政府发行债券的能力，并迫使州政府接管目前由地方政府承担的超过20亿美元的教育经费，但却没有提供支付这笔新增开支的手段。

00:01:11.870 --> 00:01:32.930
<v Bill Jerke>And so you start to add it up, and you figure out pretty quickly that the state will be short $3 to $4 billion out of a $7 billion general fund. And it's hard to understand how we're going to have a prison system, social services system, and higher education would be all but gone. It would have to virtually be all privatized at that point.
<v Bill Jerke>所以当你把这些加起来，你很快就会发现，在70亿美元的总预算中，州政府将出现30亿到40亿美元的资金缺口。而且很难理解我们将如何维持监狱系统、社会服务系统，高等教育也将几乎不复存在。到那时，它几乎必须全部私有化。

00:01:32.930 --> 00:01:39.390
<v Ira Glass>He believes the initiatives would throw Colorado into such an ongoing fiscal crisis that business would leave the state.
<v Ira Glass>他认为，这些动议将使科罗拉多州陷入持续的财政危机，导致企业纷纷撤离该州。

00:01:39.390 --> 00:01:44.560
<v Ira Glass>What do you think of the people who are trying to get these three initiatives passed?
<v Ira Glass>你对那些企图通过这三项动议的人怎么看?

00:01:44.560 --> 00:02:01.980
<v Bill Jerke>I think that they are desperate patriots. I think that they sincerely believe that government has gotten too big, and I can't disagree with them there. I just think that there are more measured ways of doing it. And they say back to me, "Yeah, but we've heard that for 20 years from you and we're tired of waiting."
<v Bill Jerke>我认为他们是绝望的爱国者。我认为他们真诚地认为政府变得太庞大了，在这点上我无法反驳他们。我只是认为有更温和的方法来做到这一点。而他们反过来对我说：“是的，但我们听你这么说20年了，我们等得不耐烦了。”

00:02:01.980 --> 00:02:12.030
<v Ira Glass>Do you think that the politics this year are just very strange, where Republicans who in the past saw eye-to-eye and worked together now are opponents on issues just like this?
<v Ira Glass>你认为今年的政治局势非常奇怪吗？过去意见一致并合作的共和党人，现在却在这样的话题上成了对手？

00:02:12.030 --> 00:02:39.950
<v Bill Jerke>I have good friends that are on the other side. They're a little bit surprised to see me on this side, I think. But when I point out the fatal flaws within these three questions, they don't have much to say to go ahead and defend themselves, either, on it. They kind of get back to just muttering again that something's got to be done to go ahead and slow down government.
<v Bill Jerke>我有好朋友站在对立面。我想，他们看到我站在这一边都有点惊讶。但当我指出这三个问题中的致命缺陷时，他们在上面也没什么话好说来为自己辩解。他们又开始嘟囔说，必须做点什么来减缓政府的发展。

00:02:39.950 --> 00:02:46.790
<v Ira Glass>This year we have Republicans impatient with the Republican party, and Democrats frustrated with the Democratic party.
<v Ira Glass>今年我们看到共和党人对共和党感到不耐烦，民主党人对民主党感到沮丧。

00:02:46.790 --> 00:02:55.120
<v Greg Shropshire>There's a lot of African Americans that are very disheartened with the Democratic party. And they see the abandonment of Barack as the abandonment of black people.
<v Greg Shropshire>有许多非裔美国人对民主党非常失望。他们将对巴拉克（奥巴马）的抛弃视作对黑人群体的抛弃。

00:02:55.120 --> 00:03:06.000
<v Ira Glass>That's Greg Shropshire of Rome, Georgia, a past president of the local chapter of a service organization called The 100 Black Men of America. He feels that the Democratic party as a whole has not stood by Barack Obama.
<v Ira Glass>这是佐治亚州罗马市的格雷格·施罗普斯和，他是当地一家名为“美国百人黑人”的服务组织分支机构的前会长。他觉得整个民主党并没有支持巴拉克·奥巴马。

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:26.870
<v Greg Shropshire>The fact that we're still discussing, 20 something months into the presidency, whether Barack is a Muslim or not is just asinine. And the fact that it's allowed, the fact that Democrats even allow people out there to even bring stuff like that up. And it's not we're going to sling mud, but the fact that you're not even speaking up for truth.
<v Greg Shropshire>在总统任期过去20多个月后，我们居然还在讨论巴拉克是不是穆斯林，这简直是愚蠢至极。而且这种现象被容忍，民主党人甚至允许人们提出这种言论。这并不是说我们要进行人身攻击，而是你们甚至没有为真相大声疾呼。

00:03:26.870 --> 00:03:58.730
<v Ira Glass>From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life , distributed by Public Radio International. I'm Ira Glass. Today on our show, on the eve of this mid-term election, people looking at their own political parties and declaring, "This party sucks." Our show today in two acts. Act one, the conservatives. Act two, the liberals. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>来自芝加哥WBEZ电台，这里是公共广播国际公司发行的《这个美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。在我们今天的节目中，在这个中期选举前夜，人们审视着他们自己的政治党派并宣告：“这个党糟透了。”我们今天的节目分为两幕。第一幕，保守派。第二幕，自由派。请继续收听。

00:03:58.730 --> 00:04:16.000
<v Ira Glass>Act one, "Patriot Games." With Tea Parties springing up around the country, so many new people are involved in politics for the first time this election, and they are getting into situations that sometimes they don't know how to handle, it's all so new. Ben Calhoun has this story of one congressional race and two friends.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕，“爱国者游戏”。随着茶党在全国各地涌现，许多新面孔在这次选举中首次涉足政治，他们陷入了有时不知该如何应对的境地，这一切都太新了。本·卡尔霍恩为您带来这个关于一场国会竞选和两位朋友的故事。

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:50.900
<v Ben Calhoun>The day Congressman Bart Stupak's seat came up for grabs was March 21, 2010, a little after 4:00. Stupak is a Democrat from Michigan. He'd been leading a group of pro-life Democrats who were holding out their votes on the health care bill until there was something in it that prevented federal money from going to abortions. They held out and held out, and then, at 4:06, Stupak announced that President Obama had agreed to issued an executive order, giving in to their demands. And because of that, Stupak's group would provide the rest of the 216 votes needed to pass the health care bill through the House.
<v Ben Calhoun>众议员巴特·斯图帕克的席位面临争夺的那天是2010年3月21日，刚过4点。斯图帕克是来自密歇根州的民主党人。他一直领导着一群反堕胎的民主党人，他们在医保法案上扣压选票，直到法案中出现防止联邦资金流向堕胎的条款。他们一再僵持，然后，在4点06分，斯图帕克宣布奥巴马总统已同意签署行政命令，向他们的要求妥协。正因如此，斯图帕克的团队将提供使医保法案在众议院通过所需的其余216票中的剩余票数。

00:04:50.900 --> 00:05:01.110
<v Bart Stupak>I am please to announce that we have an agreement, and it is with the help of the President and the Speaker, we were able to come with an agreement to protect the sanctity of life in the health care reform.
<v Bart Stupak>我很高兴地宣布，我们已经达成了一项协议，在总统和议长的帮助下，我们能够达成一项协议，以保护医疗改革中生命的尊严。

00:05:01.110 --> 00:05:05.840
<v Journalist>This puts you guys over the top? Are you now past 216?
<v Journalist>这让你们稳操胜券了吗？你们现在超过216票了吗？

00:05:05.840 --> 00:05:07.970
<v Bart Stupak>We're well past 216, yes.
<v Bart Stupak>我们远远超过216票了，是的。

00:05:07.970 --> 00:05:14.770
<v Dan Benishek>People started calling me on the phone, even as Mr. Stupak was at the podium.
<v Dan Benishek>人们开始给我打电话，甚至在斯图帕克先生还在讲台上的时候。

00:05:14.770 --> 00:05:44.060
<v Ben Calhoun>That's Dan Benishek, a surgeon, who, just a couple months before all this, had decided to run against Stupak. The day of the health care announcement, the day that Bart Stupak officially became the congressman who gave the nation Obamacare, Benishek was the only Republican challenger on the books. And there was a good reason for that. Because, after 18 years, Stupak was pretty much considered unbeatable. Essentially, you ran against him if you didn't mind losing, or if you were a total political novice like Dan Benishek.
<v Ben Calhoun>那是丹·贝尼斯海克，一名外科医生，就在这一切发生前几个月，他决定竞选挑战斯图帕克。在医保宣布的那天，在巴特·斯图帕克正式成为把“奥巴马医改”带给全国的国会议员的那天，贝尼斯海克是登记在册的唯一共和党挑战者。这背后有一个很好的理由。因为在18年之后，斯图帕克基本上被认为是不可战胜的。本质上，如果你不介意失败，或者如果你像丹·贝尼斯海克那样是个彻头彻尾的政治新手，你才会去和他竞选。

00:05:44.060 --> 00:05:56.230
<v Dan Benishek>You know, he's still there talking, answering questions and blah, blah, blah, and the phone started to ring. And it didn't stop for, I don't know, days. It rang all day and all night.
<v Dan Benishek>你知道的，他还在那里讲话、回答问题等等，然后电话就开始响了。而且响个不停，我不知道，持续了好几天。它日夜不停地响。

00:05:56.230 --> 00:05:58.530
<v Ben Calhoun>And what were people saying to you?
<v Ben Calhoun>人们都跟你说了些什么？

00:05:58.530 --> 00:06:14.830
<v Dan Benishek>They were saying, like, "How do we send you money?" [LAUGHTER] Because we didn't even have a website out. You know what I mean? I ended up raising a couple hundred thousand dollars in a matter of weeks.
<v Dan Benishek>他们说，比如，“我们怎么把钱寄给你？”[笑声]因为我们当时甚至连个网站都还没做出来。你知道我的意思吗？我最后在几周内筹集了几十万美元。

00:06:14.830 --> 00:06:16.880
<v Ben Calhoun>Did that catch you off guard?
<v Ben Calhoun>这让你措手不及吗？

00:06:16.880 --> 00:06:20.770
<v Dan Benishek>Yeah. I'll say. Unbelievably.
<v Dan Benishek>是的。我会这么说。难以置信地。

00:06:20.770 --> 00:06:53.890
<v Ben Calhoun>Pretty much overnight, Dan Benishek went from being a political nobody to being a political contender. And Bart Stupak went from being the guy who was unbeatable to being the guy who was under 24 hour police protection. Then, 19 days after he announced the health care deal, Stupak announced that he wouldn't be running again. At that moment, Michigan's first district, a conservative place we're both Democrats and Republicans are often pro-life and pro-gun, suddenly became one of the hottest commodities you can get in congressional politics-- an open seat in a swing district.
<v Ben Calhoun>几乎一夜之间，丹·贝尼斯海克从一个政治无名小卒变成了政治竞争者。而巴特·斯图帕克则从不可战胜的人变成了受到24小时警方保护的人。接着，在宣布医保协议19天后，斯图帕克宣布他将不再竞选连任。那一刻，密歇根州第一选区——一个民主党人和共和党人通常都反堕胎、支持枪支的保守地方——突然成了国会政治中最抢手的资源之一：一个摇摆选区里的空缺席位。

00:06:53.890 --> 00:07:17.460
<v Ben Calhoun>On the Democratic side, a state assemblyman named Gary McDowell jumped in to replace Stupak and ran unopposed in the Democratic primary. On the Republican side, Dan Benishek's early good luck had given him a head start, but other people climbed in, and things got nasty and contentious. And on election night, it was close. Dan Benishek stayed up late with his family, watching the votes come in.
<v Ben Calhoun>在民主党方面，一位名叫加里·麦克道尔的州议员跳出来接替斯图帕克，并在民主党初选中没有遇到对手。在共和党方面，丹·贝尼斯海克早期的好运让他占得了先机，但其他人也加入进来，事情变得糟糕而充满争议。在选举之夜，竞争非常激烈。丹·贝尼斯海克和家人熬夜很晚，看着选票不断出炉。

00:07:17.460 --> 00:07:23.160
<v Dan Benishek>It was definitely an amazing night. And I ended up winning the primary by 15 votes.
<v Dan Benishek>这绝对是一个令人惊叹的夜晚。我最终以15票的优势赢得了初选。

00:07:23.160 --> 00:07:24.870
<v Ben Calhoun>That's a squeaker.
<v Ben Calhoun>赢得真是惊险。

00:07:24.870 --> 00:07:37.900
<v Dan Benishek>You think? [LAUGHTER] We call it a "Dan slide." I know.
<v Dan Benishek>你觉得呢？[笑声]我们称之为“丹式大滑坡”。我懂的。

00:07:37.900 --> 00:08:01.480
<v Ben Calhoun>The math for this race seems pretty easy. Swing district in a Republican year. You'd figure after the primary, Dan Benishek would have it made. But it hasn't worked out that way, for pretty much one reason-- Tea Party groups. Tea Party groups have been popping up all over northern Michigan for more than a year. And, just like in other parts of the country, they've been making Republican politics more complicated.
<v Ben Calhoun>这场竞选的算术题似乎很简单。在共和党大年里的摇摆选区。你本以为初选过后，丹·贝尼斯海克就稳操胜券了。但事情并没有按那个方向发展，原因基本上只有一个——茶党团体。茶党团体在密歇根州北部如雨后春笋般涌现了一年多。而且，就像在国家其他地方一样，他们让共和党政治变得更加复杂。

00:08:01.480 --> 00:08:19.140
<v Ben Calhoun>Talking to Tea Party activists, you'll find out that most of them went to their first rally in spring of 2009. Though they're so sensitive about people saying this is really about Obama and the fact that he's black, that almost always, without fail, they'll tell you that they were upset well before Obama even took office.
<v Ben Calhoun>跟茶党积极分子交谈，你会发现他们大多数人在2009年春天参加了他们的第一次集会。尽管他们对人们说这完全是关于奥巴马及其黑人身份的说法异常敏感，以至于几乎每次、无一例外地，他们都会告诉你，早在奥巴马就职之前他们就已经很不满了。

00:08:19.140 --> 00:08:27.100
<v Rich Carlson>We started talking about forming a Tea Party even during George Bush's tenure, with the first TARP bail out.
<v Rich Carlson>早在布什执政期间推出第一笔TARP救市资金时，我们就开始讨论组建茶党了。

00:08:27.100 --> 00:09:01.930
<v Ben Calhoun>Rich Carlson lives in Petoskey, Michigan. Which, if you imagine Michigan as the back of your left hand, Petoskey would be the tip of your ring finger. Rich is a tower of a human being, a bulky six foot four. Though, because he's so good natured, he manages to be huge without ever seeming threatening. Rich, who's pushing 50, was born and raised in Petoskey, and for years he made his living there selling construction supplies. Though in 2009, with the recession, business had gotten really bad. It was around that time that Rich started talking to his friend, a guy named Tom, about starting a Tea Party.
<v Ben Calhoun>里奇·卡尔森住在密歇根州的佩托斯基。如果你把密歇根州想象成你的左手手背，佩托斯基就是你无名指的指尖。里奇身材魁梧，身高六尺四寸（约1.93米）。不过，由于他性格随和，块头虽大却从不显得具有威胁性。里奇快五十岁了，在佩托斯基出生并长大，多年来他在那里靠销售建筑材料谋生。但在2009年，由于经济衰退，生意变得非常糟糕。正是在那个时候，里奇开始和他的朋友、一个叫汤姆的人谈论建立一个茶党。

00:09:01.930 --> 00:09:20.890
<v Rich Carlson>Tom and I had been friends since kindergarten. And so, how long of a friendship is that? It's certainly a long time. Many, many fishing stories and deer hunting stories and Boy Scout campfire stories and everything else together.
<v Rich Carlson>我和汤姆从幼儿园起就是朋友了。所以，这友谊有多久了？当然是很长的时间了。有许多许多一起钓鱼的故事、打鹿的故事、童子军营火的故事以及其他所有事情。

00:09:20.890 --> 00:09:23.410
<v Tom Swenor>So the first guy I called was Rich.
<v Tom Swenor>所以我打给的第一个人就是里奇。

00:09:23.410 --> 00:09:24.730
<v Ben Calhoun>This is Tom Swenor.
<v Ben Calhoun>这是汤姆·斯韦诺。

00:09:24.730 --> 00:09:41.170
<v Tom Swenor>So sometime in March, I said, "We've got to do something." Even if it's just three guys, you know, go do something. Protest, something. Something. Because I was tired of yelling at my TV. You've probably heard that a lot.
<v Tom Swenor>所以在3月份的某个时候，我说：“我们必须做点什么。”哪怕只有三个人，你知道，去干点什么。抗议，什么都行。什么都行。因为我厌倦了对着电视机大喊大叫。你可能经常听到这样的话。

00:09:41.170 --> 00:10:09.860
<v Ben Calhoun>Like Rich, before the Tea Party, Tom had never really been involved in politics. Unlike Rich, Tom's on the short side, with a lean, stocky build and the mannerisms and personality of a vice grip. From the moment you meet him, he seems honest and practical. Tom's a land surveyor, and when construction slowed down during the recession, his business, just like Rich's, was drying up. So Tom and Rich got together with some people from Petoskey and started planning a Tea Party rally.
<v Ben Calhoun>像里奇一样，在茶党之前，汤姆从未真正涉足政治。与里奇不同的是，汤姆个子较矮，身材精瘦结实，举止和性格都像一把虎钳。从你见到他的第一刻起，他就显得诚实而务用。汤姆是一名土地测量师，当经济衰退期间建筑业放缓时，他的生意和里奇的一样，正在枯竭。于是汤姆和里奇与佩托斯基的一些人聚在一起，开始筹划一场茶党集会。

00:10:09.860 --> 00:10:29.120
<v Ben Calhoun>At some point, someone said, "Look, we need a headliner." They consider Sarah Palin, Ted Nugent, but both charge too much money. Eventually someone thought, "What about Joe the Plumber?" And then one day, Tom says, Rich was reading about a Tea Party rally in the state capital on tax day, and Joe the Plumber would be there.
<v Ben Calhoun>在某个时刻，有人说：“听着，我们需要一个领衔人物。”他们考虑过莎拉·佩林、泰德·纽根特，但两人的收费都太高了。最后有人想：“乔·水管工怎么样？”接着有一天，汤姆说，里奇在税收日那天读到一篇关于州首府茶党集会的报道，乔·水管工会去那里。

00:10:29.120 --> 00:11:06.520
<v Tom Swenor>We felt as a group, that's the guy. Nobody has any way of knowing how to get a hold of him, but Rick come up with the idea we could possibly meet him in Lansing. Rich Carlson, you know Rich, he's a lot bigger follow than I, so he couldn't get through the crowd like I could. I'm a little guy, went through there like a-- I mean, ch-ch. And I went up the stairs and I looked back on that crowd, and I snapped some really cool pictures. And that really gave me a sense of power, that this is a movement. Something is going on here.
<v Tom Swenor>我们作为一个群体觉得，就是这个人。没有人知道怎么联系到他，但里克想出了一个主意，我们可能可以在兰辛见到他。里奇·卡尔森，你认识里奇的，他比我壮得多，所以他无法像我那样穿过人群。我是个小个子，像那样穿过去——我是说，唰唰。我走上台阶，回头看着那个人群，我拍了一些非常酷的照片。这真的让我有一种力量感，觉得这是一个运动。这里正在发生一些事情。

00:11:06.520 --> 00:11:46.590
<v Tom Swenor>It was exciting to be there, but then it was also exciting because I went up into where Joe the Plumber was at. I asked a security guard. He says, "Well, he's over here." So I went, and I waited for him to get done speaking. I forget if he was talking to television people or whatever. And as soon as he come towards me, I had to say it as quick as possible, because he's just going to shuffle through the crowd. I said, "We're having a Tea Party on May 1 in Petoskey, and we want you to come to our event." And he goes, "Oh, I like Petoskey."
<v Tom Swenor>能在那里让人兴奋，但接下来的兴奋是因为我走到了乔·水管工所在的地方。我问了一个保安。他说：“哦，他在那边。”于是我去了，等他演讲结束。我忘了他是跟电视工作人员说话还是干嘛。一等他朝我走来，我必须以最快速度说出来，因为他马上就要从人群中穿过去了。我说：“我们将在5月1日在佩托斯基举办一个茶党活动，我们希望你能来参加。”他说：“哦，我喜欢佩托斯基。”

00:11:46.590 --> 00:12:12.920
<v Ben Calhoun>Joe the Plumber spoke on May 1, on a sunny Friday afternoon in Petoskey. For having just started up, the Petoskey Tea Party had hundreds of people at the rally, which is kind of remarkable considering that the city only has 6,000 people in it. Like other Tea Party rallies, the event wasn't about any candidates, or elections. The speakers that day focused on what Tea Party people call "core values" or "core principles," which we should talk about for just a second.
<v Ben Calhoun>乔·水管工在5月1日星期五下午阳光明媚的佩托斯基发表了讲话。由于刚刚成立，佩托斯基茶党集会吸引了数百人参加，考虑到该市仅有6000人口，这可以说是相当了不起。和其他茶党集会一样，该活动无关任何候选人或选举。那天的演讲者专注于茶党人所说的“核心价值观”或“核心原则”，我们应该花一秒钟讨论一下。

00:12:12.920 --> 00:12:56.940
<v Ben Calhoun>The core values are usually a few very basic things. Generally they are small government, free markets, natural rights-- meaning life, liberty, and the pursuit of happiness-- and this belief that the Constitution should be very narrowly interpreted. Talking to Tea Party people, these come up constantly, because Tea Partiers again and again go back to the idea that these values are more important than anything, more important than party politics. A lot of times, Tea Partiers tell each other that they should only support candidates who really, truly represent conservative values. An expression that gets used all the time is, "principle over party."
<v Ben Calhoun>核心价值观通常是非常基础的几样东西。一般来说，它们是小政府、自由市场、天赋人权——即生命权、自由权和追求幸福的权利——以及这种坚信宪法应该被狭义解释的信念。跟茶党人交谈时，这些话题会不断出现，因为茶党人一次又一次地回到这样一个观点：这些价值观比任何事情都重要，比党派政治更重要。很多时候，茶党人相互告诫，他们只应该支持那些真正代表保守派价值观的候选人。一个经常被使用的表达是“原则高于党派”。

00:12:56.940 --> 00:13:03.430
<v Ben Calhoun>So late this summer the race for Michigan's first district was chugging along, and one day Rich Carlson gets a phone call.
<v Ben Calhoun>所以在今年夏末，密歇根州第一选区的竞选正如火如荼地进行着，有一天里奇·卡尔森接到了一个电话。

00:13:03.430 --> 00:13:22.480
<v Rich Carlson>One day I'm the back deck of my condo, beautiful summer day. Cell phone rings. I answer the phone and I end up listening to about 20 minutes of somebody selling me on the fact of their core values and principles, and what's wrong with the country, et cetera, et cetera, et cetera.
<v Rich Carlson>有一天我在我公寓的后露台，一个美丽的夏天日子。手机响了。我接了电话，最后听了大约20分钟某人向我推销他们的核心价值观和原则，以及这个国家出了什么问题，等等，等等，等等。

00:13:22.480 --> 00:13:43.920
<v Ben Calhoun>The guy on the phone was a guy named Glenn Wilson. Wilson is a self-made millionaire who owns a telephone and internet company. Basically, Wilson was telling Rich he was thinking about getting in the race as an Independent, running on a lot of Tea Party type ideas. He was also saying he'd be willing to throw his own money into the campaign, so he wouldn't be some marginal third party guy.
<v Ben Calhoun>电话那头的人叫格伦·威尔逊。威尔逊是一个白手起家的百万富翁，拥有一家电话和互联网公司。基本上，威尔逊告诉里奇，他正考虑以独立候选人身份参选，并以很多茶党风格的理念作为竞选纲领。他还说他愿意自掏腰包投入竞选，所以他不会只是个无足轻重的第三方小角色。

00:13:43.920 --> 00:14:01.520
<v Ben Calhoun>At this point, Rich had been appointed President of the Petoskey Tea Party. Not only that, he'd co-founded an umbrella group for Tea Party groups all across northern Michigan, which was called the Northern Michigan Liberty Alliance. That day, Wilson was trying to persuade Rich to join his campaign. And Rich was up for it.
<v Ben Calhoun>此时，里奇已被任命为佩托斯基茶党的主席。不仅如此，他还共同创立了一个伞形组织，将密歇根州北部各地的茶党组织联合起来，该组织被称为密歇根州北部自由联盟。那天，威尔逊正试图说服里奇加入他的竞选团队。而里奇对此很感兴趣。

00:14:01.520 --> 00:14:15.650
<v Rich Carlson>I had a candidate that was viable, that had the financial horsepower to do it, could raise money, and had good core ideals. And so I thought, well, game on, potentially.
<v Rich Carlson>我当时有一个可行的候选人，他有这样做的资金实力，能够筹集资金，并且有很好的核心理念。所以我想，好吧，比赛可能要开始了。

00:14:15.650 --> 00:14:53.640
<v Ira Glass>Which was fine. And then Dan Benishek won the Republican primary. Which meant that Benishek would be the leading contender to beat the Democrat in November. And most of Rich's Tea Party friends were excited to support Benishek. They were psyched to have someone who could take back Stupak's seat for the conservatives. At the same time, they were worried that Glenn Wilson was going to enter the race, split the conservative vote, and spoil everything. But Rich says he was convinced. Wilson was just a better embodiment of Tea Party ideals, so he went with the Tea Party philosophy. Principal over party. Forget who's the most electable. He wasn't worried about losing the seat.
<v Ira Glass>这倒也无所谓。然后丹·贝尼斯赫克赢得了共和党初选。这意味着贝尼斯赫克将成为在11月击败民主党人的主要竞争者。里奇的大多数茶党朋友都很高兴支持贝尼斯赫克。他们为能有一个为保守派夺回斯图帕克席位的人而兴奋不已。与此同时，他们又担心格伦·威尔逊会参选，分流保守派的选票，把一切都搞砸。但里奇说他当时确信不疑。威尔逊只是茶党理念的更好化身，所以他选择了茶党哲学。原则高于党派。忘掉谁最有当选希望吧。他并不担心失去这个席位。

00:14:53.640 --> 00:15:26.990
<v Rich Carlson>There's a tremendous amount of fear of putting a Democrat in this seat. Not looking through the perspective that there's 435 seats in the US Congress and this is just one of 435 races that are happening November 2. Secondly, I believe it's going to be a landslide for the Republicans. I think that no matter how this race turns out, Independent, Republican, or Democrat, that it's still going to be a Republican-run house and more than likely a Republican-run Senate.
<v Rich Carlson>人们对让民主党人坐上这个席位有着极大的恐惧。他们没有从这样的角度来看待问题：美国国会有435个席位，这只是11月2日进行的435场竞选中的一场。其次，我相信这对共和党人来说将是一场压倒性的胜利。我认为，无论这场竞选结果如何——无论是无党派人士、共和党人还是民主党人，国会依然会由共和党掌控，参议院也大概率由共和党掌控。

00:15:26.990 --> 00:15:37.460
<v Ben Calhoun>Essentially, Rich said, if Wilson split the vote and the Democrats won the race, it might even be a good thing. Because Republicans would have to cater more to Tea Partiers the next time around.
<v Ben Calhoun>从本质上讲，里奇说，如果威尔逊分流了选票而民主党人赢得了竞选，这甚至可能是一件好事。因为共和党人在下一次选举中将不得不更多地迎合茶党人。

00:15:37.460 --> 00:15:43.580
<v Rich Carlson>This is one of the ways to return one of the parties closer to Tea Party ideals.
<v Rich Carlson>这是让其中一个政党回归茶党理念的方法之一。

00:15:43.580 --> 00:15:46.310
<v Ben Calhoun>Putting the ideals first.
<v Ben Calhoun>把理念放在首位。

00:15:46.310 --> 00:15:49.730
<v Rich Carlson>Definitely principles above party.
<v Rich Carlson>绝对是原则高于党派。

00:15:49.730 --> 00:16:10.130
<v Ben Calhoun>Rich signed on as Wilson's campaign manager, but he didn't tell anyone in the Petoskey Tea Party. He knew none of his friends would like it. They'd all talked about Wilson being a spoiler. But Rich also knew his secret could only last for so long. Tom, Rich's best friend, says it all came out in the open during the group's first meeting after the primary.
<v Ben Calhoun>里奇签约担任威尔逊的竞选经理，但他没有告诉佩托斯基茶党的任何人。他知道他的朋友们都不会喜欢这样。他们都讨论过威尔逊是个搅局者。但里奇也知道他的秘密保密不了多久。里奇最好的朋友汤姆说，这一切在初选后该团体的第一次会议上彻底公开了。

00:16:10.130 --> 00:16:36.840
<v Tom Swenor>That first meeting after the preliminary election, and I went to the Tea Party. Rich was there and, you know, it was quite a number of people. But Rich Carlson got up and walked out of the room, and this other gentleman that's there with his wife followed him. That's when I went, that's Glenn Wilson, and they're going out to coach him how to talk to us.
<v Tom Swenor>那是初选后的第一次会议，我去了茶党。里奇在那里，你知道的，有相当多的人。但是里奇·卡尔森起身走出了房间，另一个和妻子一起来的绅士也跟着他走了出去。就在那时我心想，那是格伦·威尔逊，他们出去教他怎么跟我们说话了。

00:16:36.840 --> 00:16:42.600
<v Karen Lacross>Lateron I overheard Rich say to him, Now, don't ever do this, or don't ever do that.
<v Karen Lacross>后来我无意中听到里奇对他说：“听着，永远别这么做，也永远别那么做。”

00:16:42.600 --> 00:16:51.780
<v Ben Calhoun>This is Karen LaCross, vice president of the Petoskey Tea Party, who says that night, at first, she didn't even know why Rich was giving Wilson advice.
<v Ben Calhoun>这是佩托斯基茶党副主席卡伦·拉罗斯，她说那天晚上，一开始她甚至不知道为什么里奇要在给威尔逊出谋划策。

00:16:51.780 --> 00:16:56.200
<v Karen Lacross>And then it dawned on me, oh my god, he's grooming him for candidacy.
<v Karen Lacross>然后我突然意识到，哦天哪，他在把对方培养成候选人。

00:16:56.200 --> 00:17:08.750
<v Tom Swenor>And then he introduced Glenn Wilson. He would speak, and Rich would cringe because he's saying something he's not supposed to say. And because Rich is my buddy I knew what he was up to.
<v Tom Swenor>然后他引见了格伦·威尔逊。威尔逊开始发言，而里奇则在一旁尴尬得直皱眉头，因为威尔逊说了一些他不该说的话。而且因为里奇是我的哥们儿，我知道他在搞什么鬼。

00:17:08.750 --> 00:17:26.960
<v Karen Lacross>And I thought, but just two days ago we had this discussion about we hoped he didn't enter the race. And I'm like, hm. And then later on, within days, we knew that he was working for him. And we were all shocked.
<v Karen Lacross>我想，就在两天前我们还在讨论希望他不要参选。而我当时的心情是，嗯？然后没过几天，我们就知道他在为威尔逊工作了。我们都震惊了。

00:17:26.960 --> 00:17:47.950
<v Ben Calhoun>For days after that meeting, Tom and Karen and others in the Petoskey Tea Party say they tried to get in touch with Rich. Tom called him at his house, on his cell, at the campaign. He texted him. One day, he even called a radio show and publicly went after Rich and Glenn Wilson. Karen LaCross says everyone felt betrayed. But Tom took it especially hard.
<v Ben Calhoun>在那次会议之后的几天里，汤姆、卡伦以及佩托斯基茶党的其他人说他们曾试图联系里奇。汤姆给他的家里、手机、竞选办公室打电话。他还给里奇发短信。有一天，他甚至打进一个电台节目，公开抨击里奇和格伦·威尔逊。卡伦·拉罗斯说每个人都感到被背叛了。但汤姆受到的打击尤其大。

00:17:47.950 --> 00:18:18.000
<v Karen Lacross>Tommy, being the emotional person that is, I think he took it personal. It's hard to detach yourself, especially when you have a friend like that. I think that because they're like brothers, and because they took different paths, and they think that each one has got, you know, consequences that they can't support, they took it too personal. Tom's made it very vocal, how disappointed he's been. And how how hurt he's been.
<v Karen Lacross>汤米是个感性的人，我觉得他把这事当成了个人恩怨。很难将自己抽离出来，尤其是当你拥有这样一个朋友的时候。我认为因为他们情同手足，却走上了不同的道路，并且认为对方的所作所为会带来无法承受的后果，所以他们把这事看得太个人化了。汤姆一直直言不讳地表达他的失望，以及他所受到的伤害。

00:18:18.000 --> 00:18:39.400
<v Ben Calhoun>Rick and Tom went from talking five times a day, to not speaking at all. Without an explanation from Rich, Tom now thinks the worst-- that Rich didn't take the campaign manager job because he believed in it. At this point, he says, Rich was unemployed, and he took the job for the money. Essentially, his theory is now that his best friend is a sellout.
<v Ben Calhoun>里克和汤姆从一天通五次电话，变成了完全不说话。由于里奇没有给出解释，汤姆现在往最坏处想——认为里奇接受竞选经理这份工作并不是因为他信仰这个。此时此刻，他说，里奇当时正失业，他接这份工作是为了钱。从本质上讲，他的观点现在成了他最好的朋友是个卖国求荣的叛徒。

00:18:39.400 --> 00:19:03.710
<v Tom Swenor>I guess Rich feels he's doing good in the money game. I hope to hell it was a lot. I hope it was a king's ransom, dude, I hope it wasn't just some chump change. Because I would hope that my friendship is worth 10 grand. A guy that's known him for 40 freaking years.
<v Tom Swenor>我猜里奇觉得自己在金钱游戏里干得挺好。我真他妈希望这是一大笔钱。我希望那是天价赎金，老兄，我希望这不仅仅是一些零钱。因为我希望我的友谊至少值1万块。一个认识了他整整40年的老朋友。

00:19:03.710 --> 00:19:06.670
<v Karen Lacross>We did pull an emergency meeting together.
<v Karen Lacross>我们确实召集了一次紧急会议。

00:19:06.670 --> 00:19:18.630
<v Ben Calhoun>Petoskey Tea Party Vice President Karen LaCross says, after a week of trying to get a hold of Rich and talk him out of what he was doing, she and the other board members decided enough was enough. So they called a meeting without him.
<v Ben Calhoun>佩托斯基茶党副主席卡伦·拉罗斯说，在试图联系里奇并劝他放弃正在做的事整整一周之后，她和董事会的其他成员决定够了就是够了。于是他们在没有里奇的情况下召集了一次会议。

00:19:18.630 --> 00:19:54.120
<v Karen Lacross>And it was just like, we all called each other and said, "Can you be there? Can you be there?" And we all went into a room at one of the people's offices, and we said, OK, here's the deal. Rich has been a great leader. And so we said, all right, we don't want to just blow him out of water here, because he's been an integral part of this. And so we symbolically pulled a chair up to the table that was empty and said, OK, that's Rich's chair. And all we're going to do is, we're going to swap him out temporarily with one of the regular board members, and that board member will become president pro tem.
<v Karen Lacross>感觉就像，我们互相打电话问：“你能来吗？你能去吗？”然后我们所有人都走进了其中一个人办公室里的一个房间，我们说，好吧，情况是这样的。里奇一直是个伟大的领导者。所以我们说，好吧，我们不想直接把他一棒子打死，因为他是这个组织不可或缺的一部分。因此，我们在桌旁象征性地拉了一把空椅子，说，好吧，那是里奇的椅子。我们要做的就是，临时用一位常规董事会成员替换他，该董事会成员将担任代理主席。

00:19:54.120 --> 00:19:55.680
<v Ben Calhoun>Tom was there that day.
<v Ben Calhoun>汤姆那天也在场。

00:19:55.680 --> 00:20:20.040
<v Tom Swenor>I was kind of emotionally torn. Because how much Rick was involved, but we kind of took a stand. I mean, I don't remember who made the motion and seconded it. I think there was only five of us there, six of us there. I think I was the last to vote. It was unanimous.
<v Tom Swenor>我的内心在情感上有些纠结。因为里克参与得如此之深，但我们还是表明了立场。我的意思是，我不记得是谁提出的动议并附议的。我想当时我们在场的大概只有五个人、六个人。我记得我是最后一个投票的。全票通过。

00:20:20.040 --> 00:20:33.820
<v Ben Calhoun>Even if Tom doesn't remember who it was that seconded the motion to push out Rich, records from the meeting show who it was, and it was Tom himself. For Rich, this meant he'd just been voted out of the Tea Party group he'd helped create.
<v Ben Calhoun>尽管汤姆不记得是谁附议了罢免里奇的动议，但会议记录显示了是谁，那个人正是汤姆本人。对里奇来说，这意味着他刚刚被投票驱逐出了他曾协助创建的茶党组织。

00:20:33.820 --> 00:20:47.830
<v Rich Carlson>I was notified by just standard minutes of a special meeting that was held. That I was going to be-- that an interim president was being appointed until I came off the campaign trail.
<v Rich Carlson>我是通过一次特别会议的标准会议纪要得知这一消息的。得知我将被——在我结束竞选活动之前，将任命一名临时主席。

00:20:47.830 --> 00:20:54.920
<v Ben Calhoun>And at that moment, do you remember what you thought and felt, when you when you read that?
<v Ben Calhoun>就在那一刻，当你阅读那些内容时，你还记得自己当时的想法和感受吗？

00:20:54.920 --> 00:21:10.080
<v Rich Carlson>My initial thoughts were, the GOP has taken over this Tea Party, too. That was my initial thought. Because it's happening all over the place.
<v Rich Carlson>我最初的想法是，共和党也接管了这个茶党。那是我最初的想法。因为这种事到处都在发生。

00:21:10.080 --> 00:21:25.990
<v Ben Calhoun>One of the most confusing things about the Tea Party-- and this gets talked about so much now-- is it's relationship with the GOP. Is the Tea Party just part of the Republican party? Do they want to change the Republican party? Is the Tea Party rebelling against the Republican party?
<v Ben Calhoun>关于茶党最令人困惑的事情之一——现在人们经常谈论这个——就是它与共和党的关系。茶党只是共和党的一部分吗？他们想改变共和党吗？茶党是在反叛共和党吗？

00:21:25.990 --> 00:21:52.520
<v Ben Calhoun>In all of this, it seems like the thing that you can really say about the relationship between the Republican party and the Tea Party is that it's really, really awkward. And it's awkward because there's a split in the Tea Party about how to see Republicans. There's Rich's view, which is that the Republican party has been paying lip service to conservative values. They've gone soft and should be viewed with suspicion. And Tea Partiers should be willing to walk away. Principle over party.
<v Ben Calhoun>在所有这些之中，关于共和党和茶党之间的关系，你真正能说的一点似乎是：这种关系真的、真的很尴尬。这种之所以尴尬，是因为茶党内部对于如何看待共和党存在分歧。里奇的观点是，共和党一直对保守派价值观口惠而实不至。他们已经软弱了，应当受到怀疑。茶党人应该随时准备走人。原则高于党派。

00:21:52.520 --> 00:22:13.390
<v Rich Carlson>If you get lousy service at a restaurant or a store, and you continue to go there all the time, do you think that that store or restaurant is ever going to change their level of service? No. If we just keep on voting the same way and just going with those two choices, are the parties ever going to change? No, they're not.
<v Rich Carlson>如果你在一家餐馆或商店得到了糟糕的服务，而你还一直去那里光顾，你觉得那家商店或餐馆会改变他们的服务水平吗？不会。如果我们只是继续以同样的方式投票，仅仅在这两种选择中做出抉择，政党会发生改变吗？不会，他们不会的。

00:22:13.390 --> 00:22:39.220
<v Ben Calhoun>The other Tea Party view is much more pragmatic, and it's the one that Tom has taken. It's pretty much the William F. Buckley approach-- that if you want to serve conservative ideals, you find the most electable conservative, and you gather everyone behind that candidate. And when it comes to the issues that any congressman is likely to vote on in the next two years, the Republican, Benishek, is pretty much the same as Wilson, the Independent.
<v Ben Calhoun>另一种茶党观点要务实得多，这也是汤姆所持的观点。这几乎就是小威廉·F·巴克利的路线——如果你想维护保守派的理念，你就寻找最有当选希望的保守派，然后把所有人凝聚在这位候选人身后。而当谈到任何国会议员在未来两年内可能会投票表决的议题时，共和党人贝尼斯赫克和无党派人士威尔逊几乎没有什么区别。

00:22:39.220 --> 00:22:58.710
<v Ben Calhoun>For Rich and Tom, the split has been so severe that Tom can't even understand where Rich is coming from. And without an explanation from Rich, his theories have gotten pretty harsh. At this point, his theory is that Glenn Wilson is being bribed with stimulus money to spoil the race, and Rich got duped.
<v Ben Calhoun>对于里奇和汤姆来说，这种分裂是如此严重，以至于汤姆甚至无法理解里奇的想法。在没有里奇解释的情况下，他的推测变得相当苛刻。此时此刻，他的推测是格伦·威尔逊正拿着刺激经济计划的资金受贿来搅局，而里奇被蒙蔽了。

00:22:58.710 --> 00:23:29.160
<v Tom Swenor>Never once did our Tea Party say, we're going to be the spoiler and we're going to help the Democrat win. That was never in our discussion. Shame on you guys. How come he couldn't see through Glenn Wilson like everybody did the first time we met him? You're a spoiler. You're a spoiler, and you're a spoiler. Glenn Wilson, you're a spoiler. You've been put here for a reason, and not all good reason.
<v Tom Swenor>我们的茶党从来没有说过，我们要当搅局者，我们要帮助民主党人获胜。这从未在我们的讨论中出现过。真丢脸，你们这群人。为什么他不像我们第一次见到格伦·威尔逊时那样，一眼看穿他的本质？你是个搅局者。你是个搅局者，你也是个搅局者。格伦·威尔逊，你是个搅局者。把你安插在这里是有原因的，而且绝不是什么好原因。

00:23:29.160 --> 00:24:09.660
<v Ben Calhoun>I told Tom what Rich said, that there's 435 seats in Congress, and it might be a wake-up call if the Tea Party costs the Republicans a seat or two. Tom said Michigan's first congressional district race, it's not just any seat. It's a symbol. Because it was Stupak that allowed the health care bill to pass. Tom says he has fantasies about Dan Benishek taking a seat and then someday casting the vote that repeals the health care bill. "Wouldn't that just be so beautiful?" he said. And all Glenn Wilson can do is ruin that.
<v Ben Calhoun>我把里奇的话告诉了汤姆，里奇说国会有435个席位，如果茶党让共和党人丢掉一两个席位，这可能会起到一个警钟的作用。汤姆说，密歇根州第一国会选区的竞选可不是随便什么席位。这是一个象征。因为正是斯图帕克让医改法案得以通过。汤姆说他脑海中常有一些幻想：丹·贝尼斯赫克赢得席位，然后在某天投下废除医改法案的那一票。“难道这不正是最美好的事情吗？”他说。而格伦·威尔逊所能做的只有毁掉这一切。

00:24:09.660 --> 00:24:36.180
<v Ben Calhoun>According to the latest poll, the race between the Republican, Dan Benishek, and the Democrat, Gary McDowell, now a dead heat, each of them holding about 40% of the vote. Rich's candidate, Glenn Wilson, is getting 8% of the vote. Meaning he could actually be a spoiler and throw the race to the Democrat. That fact has become super stressful for a lot of Tea Party type voters, who account for many of the people supporting him.
<v Ben Calhoun>根据最新民调，共和党人丹·贝尼斯赫克和民主党人加里·麦克道威尔之间的竞选现在势均力敌，两人的支持率各自约为40%。里奇支持的候选人格伦·威尔逊获得了8%的支持率。这意味着他实际上可能会成为搅局者，把选票拱手让给民主党。这个事实给许多支持他的茶党选民带来了巨大的压力。

00:24:36.180 --> 00:24:55.010
<v Ben Calhoun>At the beginning of the month, the Tea Party umbrella group that Rich Carlson co-founded, the Northern Michigan Liberty Alliance, got together to talk about all this. About how people were divided, divided over the question about what exactly they should do. They met on a Saturday afternoon, at a Quality Inn next to a freeway ramp.
<v Ben Calhoun>在这个月初，由里奇·卡尔森共同创立的茶党伞形组织——密歇根北部自由联盟聚集在一起讨论这一切。讨论人们是如何产生分歧的，在到底应该做什么这个问题上意见不一。他们在星期六下午开会，地点在高速公路匝道旁的一家速8酒店（Quality Inn）。

00:24:55.010 --> 00:25:04.080
<v Man>OK. Thank you for coming. This place still cost us money. Anybody would like to donate a couple of bucks in our can, we would appreciate that.
<v Man>好的。谢谢你们的到来。这个地方还是要花我们的钱的。如果有谁想在我们的捐款箱里捐上几块钱，我们将不胜感激。

00:25:04.080 --> 00:25:20.700
<v Ben Calhoun>About 40 people from Tea Party groups from all around northern Michigan were there that day. Within the first half an hour, one guy stood up and stated the whole dilemma pretty plainly. He said recently he'd read a book about Libertarianism and in the back of the book, there was a quiz. So he took it.
<v Ben Calhoun>那天来自密歇根州北部各地茶党组织的约40人出席了会议。在头半小时内，有一名男子站起来，相当直白地陈述了整个困境。他说自己最近读了一本关于自由意志主义的书，书的背面有一份测试题。于是他就做了测试。

00:25:20.700 --> 00:25:51.110
<v Man>I was surprised to find out that I am 100% Libertarian in philosophy. My question is, even though I might like to be a Libertarian, I would also like to have an influence. And it seems like we've got a problem to really make something happen. And so, as such, I don't know anything better I can do at the a moment than to vote Republican. I don't like that, but what do we do?
<v Man>我很惊讶地发现，在哲学上我是一个100%的自由意志主义者。我的问题是，尽管我可能想成为一名自由意志主义者，但我也希望能有一些影响力。而且看起来我们很难真正促成一些事情发生。因此，就目前而言，除了投票给共和党，我不知道还能做什么更好的事情。我不喜欢这样，但我们能怎么办呢？

00:25:51.110 --> 00:26:04.400
<v Ben Calhoun>Again and again, the room felt like a bunch of independent-minded idealists coming up against the hard political reality that the Republican party, the one so many of them resent, is there only real viable way to be relevant.
<v Ben Calhoun>一次又一次，这个房间里给人的感觉就像是一群思想独立的理想主义者，在严酷的政治现实面前碰壁——那个他们许多人深恶痛绝的共和党，却是他们能够保持政治影响力的唯一真正可行的途径。

00:26:04.400 --> 00:26:31.240
<v Ben Calhoun>Through all this, Rich Carlson sat at the front of the room, so quiet you wouldn't have known he was there. Then, at one point, Karen LaCross, the Vice President of the Petoskey Tea Party, Rich's old group, spoke up. Karen said it was possible for the Tea Party to take sides with the Republicans without being co-opted. She singled out Chad Stevens, this really thoughtful Libertarian guy who had co-founded the Northern Michigan Liberty Alliance with Rich.
<v Ben Calhoun>经历了这一切，里奇·卡尔森坐在房间的前面，安静得你都感觉不到他在那儿。后来，在某个时刻，里奇旧日的组织——佩托斯基茶党的副主席卡伦·拉罗斯发了言。卡伦说，茶党有可能在不被同化的前提下站在共和党一边。她点名提到了查德·斯蒂芬斯（Chad Stevens），一个非常深思熟虑的自由意志主义者，他曾与里奇共同创立了密歇根北部自由联盟。

00:26:31.240 --> 00:26:55.840
<v Karen Lacross>Do not implode on each other. That's my concern, is we're all sitting here trying to poke each other and say, "You're wrong. No, you're wrong. No, you're wrong." We can't do that any more. And Chad, if you want to vote for a third-party candidate, you know what? That's your zen. You know, that's what you're-- But, the bottom line is, don't sit there and poke each other in the eyes. We're not making any ground.
<v Karen Lacross>不要内部瓦解。这是我所担心的，我们都坐在这里试图互相攻击，然后说：“你错了。不，你错了。不，你错了。”我们不能再这样下去了。查德，如果你想投票给第三方候选人，你知道吗？那是你的气场。你知道，那就是你的选择——但是，最底线是，不要坐在那里互相往对方眼睛里戳。我们这样什么也进展不了。

00:26:55.840 --> 00:27:26.720
<v Chad Stevens>I'd say this, and I agree with what you said. The attacks on people, people like Rich Carlson have been tarred and feathered for advancing liberty the way he best sees fit. If we all want to vote for Dan Benishek, the Republican, great. But if 10%, 15% want to advance liberty, and they're sick of the two party system? Fine, that's their call. The GOP has only itself to blame for people looking elsewhere. For years they have thrown liberty under the bus.
<v Chad Stevens>我要说的是，我同意你刚才所说的话。针对个人的攻击，像里奇·卡尔森这样的人，因为以他认为最合适的方式推进自由而遭到了严厉的百般嘲弄和诋毁。如果我们所有人都想投票给共和党人丹·贝尼斯赫克，那很好。但如果有10%、15%的人想要推进自由，而且他们对两党制感到厌倦了？很好，那是他们的决定。共和党人落得今天这步田地，只能怪他们自己。多年来，他们一直把自由抛诸脑后。

00:27:26.720 --> 00:27:28.240
<v Chad Stevens>[APPLAUSE]
<v Chad Stevens>[掌声]

00:27:28.240 --> 00:27:49.350
<v Ben Calhoun>After about five hours if this, someone from the Glenn Wilson campaign-- that's Rich's guy-- they whispered to me that Glenn was outside and he wanted to talk to me. I walked out and around the corner to the parking lot, where Glenn's campaign bus was idling. It's this big silver deal, with a huge photo of Glenn's face, and a big bald eagle on the side.
<v Ben Calhoun>大约进行了五个小时的这类讨论后，格伦·威尔逊竞选团队里的一个人——也就是里奇支持的那拨人——低声对我说，格伦在外面，他想跟我谈谈。我走了出去，绕过拐角来到停车场，格伦的竞选大巴正空转着停在那里。这是一辆巨大的银色大巴，车身印着格伦巨大的面部照片，侧面还有一只巨大的秃鹰。

00:27:49.350 --> 00:28:03.910
<v Ben Calhoun>A couple days before all this, I'd spent a day on the bus. And just to give you a little sense of him, Glenn is in his 40s, but he looks 30. And he drinks Mountain Dew relentlessly. I saw him drink seven or eight cans in an afternoon.
<v Ben Calhoun>在这之前几天，我在大巴上度过了一天。为了让你对他的情况有个大致了解，格伦虽然已经四十多岁了，但看起来像三十岁。而且他没完没了地喝激浪（Mountain Dew）汽水。我看到他在一个下午就喝了七八罐。

00:28:03.910 --> 00:28:06.000
<v Ben Calhoun>What's up you guys? How you been?
<v Ben Calhoun>你们好啊？最近怎么样？

00:28:06.000 --> 00:28:08.080
<v Glenn Wilson>Hey, Ben, long time no see.
<v Glenn Wilson>嘿，本，好久不见。

00:28:08.080 --> 00:28:09.520
<v Ben Calhoun>Yeah, hey, how are you?
<v Ben Calhoun>对，嘿，你好吗？

00:28:09.520 --> 00:28:11.560
<v Glenn Wilson>So the Tea Party break up?
<v Glenn Wilson>所以茶党分裂了？

00:28:11.560 --> 00:28:12.420
<v Ben Calhoun>So what's up?
<v Ben Calhoun>那么，怎么回事？

00:28:12.420 --> 00:28:13.870
<v Glenn Wilson>Are we on the air?
<v Glenn Wilson>我们在录音吗？

00:28:13.870 --> 00:28:16.050
<v Ben Calhoun>Yeah. I thought that's what you wanted.
<v Ben Calhoun>对。我以为这是你想要的。

00:28:16.050 --> 00:28:17.590
<v Glenn Wilson>I know. Shut it off for a minute.
<v Glenn Wilson>我知道。先关掉一会儿。

00:28:17.590 --> 00:28:19.090
<v Ben Calhoun>OK.
<v Ben Calhoun>好的。

00:28:19.090 --> 00:28:43.410
<v Ira Glass>In 10 years of covering politics, I've never had any politician do something quite as bizarre as what Glenn Wilson did next. He told me he wanted to fire Rich. Then he asked me what I thought of it. I told him that I didn't have an opinion, and even if I did, it wouldn't be appropriate for me to share it. And then I told him that if this is why he called me out there, I would need him to say it on tape.
<v Ira Glass>在我报道政治的10年里，我从没见过哪个政客做出像格伦·威尔逊接下来那样古怪的举动。他告诉我他想解雇里奇。然后他问我对此怎么看。我告诉他我没有看法，即便有，由我分享也不太合适。接着我告诉他，如果这就是他把我叫到这里的原因，我需要他把这话录下来。

00:28:43.410 --> 00:28:52.500
<v Ben Calhoun>If that's what you wanted to tell me, then we're rolling.
<v Ben Calhoun>如果你想说的就是这个，那我们现在就开始录音。

00:28:52.500 --> 00:29:11.570
<v Glenn Wilson>I think I need to relieve Rich of his position as campaign manager and install a new campaign manager. We need to find a campaign manager that's able to organize and manage in a much more efficient way than Rich can. Or has.
<v Glenn Wilson>我想我需要解除里奇作为竞选经理的职务，并任命一位新的竞选经理。我们需要找一位比里奇更有组织和管理能力的竞选经理，能以高得多的效率开展工作。或者说比他过去做的好。

00:29:11.570 --> 00:29:15.320
<v Ben Calhoun>Well, when are you going to tell him?
<v Ben Calhoun>好吧，你打算什么时候告诉他？

00:29:15.320 --> 00:29:20.650
<v Glenn Wilson>As soon as he comes out of this meeting.
<v Glenn Wilson>他一开完这个会就说。

00:29:20.650 --> 00:29:30.410
<v Ben Calhoun>After the meeting, Wilson grabbed Rich, and he led him down a hallway past the hotel bar, into a small conference room. I waited in the hall.
<v Ben Calhoun>会后，威尔逊抓起里奇，带着他穿过走廊，走过酒店酒吧，进入一个小型会议室。我在大厅里等着。

00:29:30.410 --> 00:29:58.160
<v Ben Calhoun>Back on the bus, Glenn had shown me the campaign calendar and said he was going to fire Rich because he hadn't set up enough events. What I didn't say to him then was that I had spent time with the other campaigns, and their schedules weren't very different, though maybe Glenn was just too inexperienced to know that. After about 10 minutes, Glenn walked out. As he passed me, he winked and said, "Thanks, have a good one." So I went to talk to Rich.
<v Ben Calhoun>在巴士上，格伦曾向我展示竞选日程表，并说他要解雇里奇，因为里奇没有安排足够多的活动。我当时没有对他说的是，我花时间了解过其他阵营，他们的日程安排其实也差不了多少，也许格伦只是太缺乏经验，才没看出这一点。大约10分钟后，格伦走了出来。他从我身边经过时，眨了眨眼说：“谢谢，祝你过得愉快。”于是我去找里奇谈话。

00:29:58.160 --> 00:29:58.660
<v Rich Carlson>Yes, sir.
<v Rich Carlson>是的，长官。

00:29:58.650 --> 00:30:00.290
<v Ben Calhoun>Hey.
<v Ben Calhoun>嘿。

00:30:00.290 --> 00:30:02.030
<v Rich Carlson>Yes.
<v Rich Carlson>好的。

00:30:02.030 --> 00:30:02.840
<v Ben Calhoun>How's it going?
<v Ben Calhoun>最近怎么样？

00:30:02.840 --> 00:30:07.960
<v Rich Carlson>Wonderful. Am I on tape? OK, good.
<v Rich Carlson>好极了。我在录音吗？好的，不错。

00:30:07.960 --> 00:30:17.930
<v Ben Calhoun>Glenn had taken away Rich's briefcase, computer, and his phone, which all belonged to the campaign, and Rich stood there, packing his things into a plastic shopping bag.
<v Ben Calhoun>格伦拿走了里奇的公文包、电脑和手机——这些都属于竞选团队——而里奇就站在那里，把自己的东西打包进一个塑料购物袋里。

00:30:17.930 --> 00:30:31.900
<v Rich Carlson>Total surprise to me, so. It has nothing to do with politics, just organization. So that's the way it goes.
<v Rich Carlson>对我来说完全是意料之外，所以。这跟政治无关，纯粹是组织问题。事情就是这样。

00:30:31.900 --> 00:30:37.470
<v Ben Calhoun>So, can you tell me about the conversation that you just had?
<v Ben Calhoun>那么，你能告诉我你们刚刚进行的谈话吗？

00:30:37.470 --> 00:30:40.000
<v Rich Carlson>No.
<v Rich Carlson>不能。

00:30:40.000 --> 00:30:41.600
<v Ben Calhoun>Why not?
<v Ben Calhoun>为什么不呢？

00:30:41.600 --> 00:30:52.300
<v Rich Carlson>Maybe in a day or two I can, sure. It just happened moments ago, so I'm tired. I've had a long day, and I'm looking forward to going home.
<v Rich Carlson>也许过一两天可以，当然。这刚刚才发生，所以我很累。我度过了漫长的一天，现在我只想回家。

00:30:52.300 --> 00:30:55.310
<v Ben Calhoun>I have to say, I feel really bad for you.
<v Ben Calhoun>我得说，我真的替你感到难过。

00:30:55.310 --> 00:31:00.040
<v Rich Carlson>No, don't feel bad for me at all. No.
<v Rich Carlson>不，一点也不用替我难过。不。

00:31:00.040 --> 00:31:09.020
<v Ben Calhoun>Rich had lost the Tea Party group he'd started the year before. He lost his best friend. And now, he'd just lost the job he'd given it all up for.
<v Ben Calhoun>里奇失去了他前一年一手创立的茶党组织。他失去了最好的朋友。而现在，他又失去了他为此放弃一切的工作。

00:31:09.020 --> 00:31:15.270
<v Ben Calhoun>I mean, you seem like you've given up a lot to be doing what you're doing right now.
<v Ben Calhoun>我是说，你看上去为了做现在做的事，放弃了很多东西。

00:31:15.270 --> 00:31:23.110
<v Rich Carlson>More than what most people realize. But I still believe in the cause.
<v Rich Carlson>比大多数人意识到的还要多。但我依然信仰这个事业。

00:31:23.110 --> 00:31:24.760
<v Ben Calhoun>What do you mean?
<v Ben Calhoun>你这话是什么意思？

00:31:24.760 --> 00:31:59.620
<v Rich Carlson>Well, I believe that it's very important to push forth an independent candidate. Am I crushed or disappointed? Yeah, I'm disappointed I couldn't see it to the end, but I'll still be involved. I'm still supportive of it. I'm still supportive of the campaign. I think it's great to give people a viable option besides the normal two party system. For once they've got somebody that's running that can actually win this thing.
<v Rich Carlson>嗯，我相信推出一位独立候选人非常重要。我是不是心碎了或者失望了？对，我没能看到它走到最后，我确实感到失望，但我仍会参与其中。我仍然支持它。我仍然支持这场竞选。我认为给人们除了传统的两党体制之外一个切实可行的选择，这是一件很棒的事。破天荒地，他们有了一个正在参选并且真的能赢得这场竞选的人。

00:31:59.620 --> 00:32:10.590
<v Ben Calhoun>A couple days after he got fired, I met with Rich one more time before leaving Michigan, just to talk about how he was feeling and what was happening now. Then, while we were sitting there, Rich got a phone call.
<v Ben Calhoun>在他被解雇几天后，我在离开密歇根之前又和里奇见了一面，只是聊聊他的感受以及现在的情况。然后，当我们坐在那里时，里奇接到了一个电话。

00:32:10.590 --> 00:32:14.470
<v Rich Carlson>Local Tea Party-- [PHONE RINGS] Sorry.
<v Rich Carlson>当地茶党——[电话铃声]抱歉。

00:32:14.470 --> 00:32:20.240
<v Ben Calhoun>The call was from a guy named Tony Cutler, a campaign staffer for the Republican candidate, Dan Benishek.
<v Ben Calhoun>这通电话来自一个叫托尼·卡特勒的人，他是共和党候选人丹·贝尼斯赫克的竞选工作人员。

00:32:20.240 --> 00:32:21.590
<v Rich Carlson>Tony, how you doing?
<v Rich Carlson>托尼，近来可好？

00:32:21.590 --> 00:32:24.000
<v Tony Cutler>Richard, what are you doing?
<v Tony Cutler>理查德，你在干什么呢？

00:32:24.000 --> 00:32:26.020
<v Rich Carlson>You don't want to know.
<v Rich Carlson>你肯定不想知道。

00:32:26.020 --> 00:32:29.680
<v Tony Cutler>I heard you were released from the Glenn Wilson campaign.
<v Tony Cutler>我听说你被格伦·威尔逊阵营开除了。

00:32:29.680 --> 00:32:32.440
<v Rich Carlson>Oh douche bag. How have you been?
<v Rich Carlson>噢，你个混蛋。你最近怎么样？

00:32:32.440 --> 00:32:34.650
<v Tony Cutler>Well, good, how are you?
<v Tony Cutler>挺好的，你呢？

00:32:34.650 --> 00:32:42.680
<v Rich Carlson>I'm doing great. Released. We had a difference of opinions. How about if I leave it at that?
<v Rich Carlson>我挺好的。被开除了。我们意见不合。我就说到这儿，行吗？

00:32:42.680 --> 00:32:58.400
<v Tony Cutler>So let's get you out on the side of bashing him by saying you left him and are against his campaign. And we'll make you look good for leaving, and we'll spin it.
<v Tony Cutler>那我们让你站出来抨击他，就说你是主动离开他的，并且反对他的竞选。我们会把你塑造成主动离开的正义形象，然后我们来操纵舆论。

00:32:58.400 --> 00:33:23.880
<v Ben Calhoun>Tony gave me permission to broadcast this conversation, though he asked that I say that he was not calling Rich on behalf of the Benishek campaign. Which seemed like a strange request, because in the call, it was pretty clear that he was. He was telling Rich that Rich should put out a press release, and he should lie. He should say that he hadn't been fired. Instead, he'd quit out of principal because Wilson was going to be a spoiler.
<v Ben Calhoun>托尼允许我播出这段对话，不过他要求我说他打电话给里奇并不是代表贝尼斯赫克阵营。这似乎是个奇怪的要求，因为在通话中，情况已经非常明显了。他告诉里奇，里奇应该发表一份新闻稿，而且他应该撒谎。他应该说自己不是被解雇的。相反，他是出于原则主动辞职的，因为威尔逊会成为一个搅局者。

00:33:23.880 --> 00:33:27.010
<v Rich Carlson>Yeah. That's something that you and I probably need to talk about.
<v Rich Carlson>对。这可能是你和我需要谈谈的事情。

00:33:27.010 --> 00:33:28.230
<v Tony Cutler>Huh?
<v Tony Cutler>啊？

00:33:28.230 --> 00:33:31.100
<v Rich Carlson>That's probably something you and I need to talk about.
<v Rich Carlson>这可能确实是你和我需要谈谈的事情。

00:33:31.100 --> 00:34:05.520
<v Tony Cutler>Well, we need to talk about it right this second, because I've got people writing two stories. We've got Benishek people writing the story saying you were fired, to make it look like Glenn Wilson doesn't know what he's doing. Or we can make it look like  you realized that this guy is  no good, he has no vision, he's only in it for himself. And you were supporting him on principal, and now that he's abandoned his principals, let's get the GOP elected and the party back on track. I want to try to save your image as much as we can here.
<v Tony Cutler>好吧，我们现在就要谈这事，因为我有人在写两篇不同的报道。贝尼斯赫克的人在写一篇报道，说你是被解雇的，好让格伦·威尔逊看起来像是个不知轻重的人。或者我们可以把它塑造成你意识到这家伙不是好东西，他没有远见，他只为自己谋利。而你以前是出于原则支持他，现在既然他背弃了原则，那就让我们把共和党选上去，让党回归正轨。我在这里想尽一切办法尽量挽救你的形象。

00:34:05.520 --> 00:34:06.960
<v Rich Carlson>Right.
<v Rich Carlson>对。

00:34:06.960 --> 00:34:19.860
<v Tony Cutler>The article will be written by the news. It will read that Wilson fires his top aide, and he'll get to control the story by saying, you know, the guy didn't know what he was doing--
<v Tony Cutler>这篇文章将由新闻媒体撰写。内容会是威尔逊解雇了他的头号助手，他将通过声称——你知道的——这家伙根本不知道自己在干什么，来掌控故事的发展方向——

00:34:19.860 --> 00:34:20.790
<v Rich Carlson>Yes.
<v Rich Carlson>是的。

00:34:20.790 --> 00:34:31.340
<v Tony Cutler>Or you come out-- I can get you in contact with the press people, and you can come out as the guy saying, you know, this is not what it's about.
<v Tony Cutler>或者你站出来——我可以帮你联系媒体人员，然后你可以作为当事人站出来说，你知道的，事情根本不是这样的。

00:34:31.340 --> 00:34:47.510
<v Ben Calhoun>Slowly, over the course of the next 15 minutes, Rich caved. He said he didn't want to be part of anything that criticized Glenn Wilson, but he would go along with Tony's plan, to put out something saying Wilson was a spoiler for conservatives, and he should drop out.
<v Ben Calhoun>慢慢地，在接下来的15分钟里，里奇屈服了。他说他不想参与任何批评格伦·威尔逊的事情，但愿配合托尼的计划，发表一份声明，称威尔逊对保守派来说是个搅局者，他应该退选。

00:34:47.510 --> 00:34:54.140
<v Rich Carlson>You can craft that, and then are you going to say that the Benishek campaign talked to me?
<v Rich Carlson>你可以拟定这个声明，然后你要说贝尼斯赫克阵营找过我吗？

00:34:54.140 --> 00:34:55.560
<v Tony Cutler>Yeah.
<v Tony Cutler>对。

00:34:55.560 --> 00:34:56.380
<v Rich Carlson>OK.
<v Rich Carlson>好的。

00:34:56.380 --> 00:34:58.660
<v Tony Cutler>All right, I'll keep in touch with you today.
<v Tony Cutler>行，我今天会跟你保持联系的。

00:34:58.660 --> 00:35:03.840
<v Rich Carlson>Please do, thank you. See you, bye.
<v Rich Carlson>请务必保持，谢谢。再见，拜拜。

00:35:03.840 --> 00:35:31.420
<v Ben Calhoun>After he hung up, I asked Rich about what he'd said to me before, all the things about principal over party. We went back and forth again and again, because he was saying these totally contradictory things. One minute he'd say the thing he'd been telling me all along. Tea Party people need to be independent, even if that means spoiling races for Republicans. Then, the next minute, he'd say the thing he'd agree to with Tony, that Wilson would be a spoiler and he should get out.
<v Ben Calhoun>挂断电话后，我问起里奇之前对我说过的话，那些关于“原则高于党派”的高论。我们一次又一次地反复辩论，因为他说的话完全自相矛盾。前一分钟他还坚持他一直以来对我说的那些话：茶党人必须保持独立，哪怕这意味着搅黄共和党的选情。而紧接着下一分钟，他又说出他答应托尼的话——威尔逊会是个搅局者，他应该退选。

00:35:31.420 --> 00:35:35.410
<v Rich Carlson>First and foremost, I don't want to see the Democrats take this seat.
<v Rich Carlson>首先也是最重要的一点，我不想看到民主党人拿下这个席位。

00:35:35.410 --> 00:35:54.760
<v Ben Calhoun>But, essentially what you had said to me before was, if an independent candidate with better core values stopped the GOP from winning this race that by the numbers, by the math, they should win, they would have incentive to listen to folks in the Liberty movement.
<v Ben Calhoun>但本质上你之前对我说过的是，如果一个拥有更好核心价值观的独立候选人，阻止了共和党赢得这场按常理、按数据本该由他们赢下的竞选，他们就会有动力去倾听自由运动中民众的声音。

00:35:54.760 --> 00:36:03.400
<v Rich Carlson>Unfortunately, that-- I'm not being given that opportunity anymore by the Wilson campaign.
<v Rich Carlson>不幸的是，那个——威尔逊阵营不再给我这个机会了。

00:36:03.400 --> 00:36:05.420
<v Ben Calhoun>Wait. I don't understand what you're saying.
<v Ben Calhoun>等等。我听不懂你在说什么。

00:36:05.420 --> 00:36:14.520
<v Rich Carlson>Well, we've reached a decision that I'm no longer with the campaign. So that is not an option for me anymore on a personal level.
<v Rich Carlson>嗯，我们已经达成共识，我不再效力于该竞选团队了。所以在个人层面上，这对我来说已经不再是一个选项了。

00:36:14.520 --> 00:36:17.970
<v Ben Calhoun>But that doesn't affect how you feel about it, right?
<v Ben Calhoun>但这并不影响你的真实感受，对吧？

00:36:17.970 --> 00:36:24.600
<v Rich Carlson>Maybe you and I should discuss this a little bit without the microphone being on. I guess I'm trying to understand which direction you're taking here.
<v Rich Carlson>也许你和我应该在不录音的情况下讨论一下这个。我想我是在努力理解你现在到底想走哪个方向。

00:36:24.600 --> 00:36:34.960
<v Ben Calhoun>I'm just trying to rectify what you said to me. You were saying that you're not saying he should pull out, but essentially what you just told Tony was that he should pull out.
<v Ben Calhoun>我只是想理清你对我说过的话。你之前说你不是在叫他退选，但你刚才对托尼说的话本质上就是叫他退选。

00:36:34.960 --> 00:36:41.230
<v Rich Carlson>But I still believe that he probably should stay in the race just to rub it in the Republicans' nose.
<v Rich Carlson>但从个人角度，我仍然认为他大概应该留在竞选中，好给共和党人添堵。

00:36:41.230 --> 00:36:43.030
<v Ben Calhoun>Just personally?
<v Ben Calhoun>纯粹是个人想法？

00:36:43.030 --> 00:36:58.950
<v Rich Carlson>Yeah, personally. But if I have a future in politics, don't you think it's wise that I say, hey, I got out of it, and he continued on?
<v Rich Carlson>对，个人想法。但如果我在政坛还有未来，难道你不觉得我声明“我退出了，而他继续参选”是明智之举吗？

00:36:58.950 --> 00:37:02.290
<v Ben Calhoun>You know what's funny? I feel like that's the most cynical thing I've heard you say.
<v Ben Calhoun>你知道最搞笑的是什么吗？我觉得这是我听你说过最冷血 cynical 的话。

00:37:02.290 --> 00:37:08.390
<v Rich Carlson>Is that cynical? Is that cynical?
<v Rich Carlson>这叫冷血吗？这叫冷血吗？

00:37:08.390 --> 00:37:15.420
<v Ben Calhoun>A little bit, don't you think?
<v Ben Calhoun>多少有一点吧，你不觉得吗？

00:37:15.420 --> 00:37:19.860
<v Rich Carlson>I think that's reality.
<v Rich Carlson>我觉得这就是现实。

00:37:19.860 --> 00:37:42.870
<v Ben Calhoun>Not 10 hours later, I got an email from the Dan Benishek campaign. It was a forwarded press release with Rich's name on it. It read, quote, "Congressional candidate Glenn Wilson is running without the support of his top aide today. Campaign manager Rich Carlson made the decision to step down from the top post, as a result of a difference in opinions with the candidate."
<v Ben Calhoun>不到10个小时后，我收到了来自丹·贝尼斯赫克竞选团队的电子邮件。那是一封转发的新闻稿，上面有里奇的名字。上面写道：“国会候选人格伦·威尔逊今天在失去其头号助手支持的情况下继续参选。竞选经理里奇·卡尔森因与候选人意见不合，决定辞去最高职务。”

00:37:42.870 --> 00:38:17.400
<v Ben Calhoun>A couple weeks after all this happened, Rich told me he still feels like he did the right thing. He said if anything's changed, it's that he's not sure how much he wants to be involved in politics. I asked both Rich and Tom the other day if they'd started talking again, and they both said no. When I asked each of them if they were planning to call, they also gave me the same answer. Each said they weren't going to, because they weren't the person who did something wrong.
<v Ben Calhoun>这一切发生几周后，里奇告诉我，他仍然觉得自己的做法没有错。他说，如果有什么改变的话，那就是他不确定自己到底还想不想涉足政治。前几天我问里奇和汤姆是否恢复了联系，他们都说没有。当我问他们各自是否打算给对方打电话时，他们也给出了同样的答案。双方都说他们不会打，因为做错事的又不是自己。

00:38:17.400 --> 00:38:38.690
<v Ira Glass>Ben Calhoun in one of the producers of our show. Coming up, to paraphrase Professor Henry Higgins in My Fair Lady , why can't a Democrat be more like a Republican? That's in a minute from Chicago Public Radio and Public Radio International, when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>本·卡尔houn是我们节目的制作人之一。接下来，借用《窈窕淑女》中亨利·希金斯教授的话来说，为什么民主党人不能表现得更像个共和党人呢？稍后将由芝加哥公共广播电台和国际公共广播电台继续播出我们的节目。

00:38:38.690 --> 00:38:58.280
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life , I'm Ira Glass. Each week on our show, of course, we choose some theme, bring you a variety of different kinds of stories on that theme. Today's show, "This Party Sucks." Stories for this mid-term election of political people who have problems with their own political parties. We've arrived at Act two of our show. Act Two, "Mister Hitt Goes to Washington."
<v Ira Glass>这里是《美国生活》，我是艾拉·格拉斯。当然，每周我们的节目都会选择一个主题，为您带来各种不同类型的相关故事。今天的节目是“这个党糟透了”。这些故事讲述了在本次中期选举中，那些对自己的政党感到头疼的政界人士。我们的节目来到了第二幕。第二幕，“希特先生去华盛顿”。

00:38:58.280 --> 00:39:18.160
<v Ira Glass>It goes without saying at this point that lots of people around the country are unhappy with the legislation that the President has pushed, the substance of what the President has done. But when you talk to so many Democrats, whatever qualms they may have with the actual programs the President has, those are overshadowed by their feelings about the President's own inability to sell his programs.
<v Ira Glass>此时此刻不言而喻的是，全国上下许多人都对总统极力推动的立法以及总统所作所为的实质内容感到不满。但当你与众多民主党人交谈时，无论他们对总统实际推行的方案有什么疑虑，这些疑虑都被他们对总统自身无法推销其方案的无能所引发的情绪给掩盖了。

00:39:18.160 --> 00:39:26.180
<v Ira Glass>A small businessman from Pennsylvania named Walt Rowen, who likes the President's programs, complained about this directly to the President's face at one of his recent town meetings.
<v Ira Glass>宾夕法尼亚州的一位小企业主沃尔特·罗恩很喜欢总统的项目，他在最近的一次市民大会上，当着总统的面直接抱怨了这一点。

00:39:26.180 --> 00:39:48.560
<v Walt Rowen>Your stimulus package actually funded a very ailing financial system, which was essential for small businesspeople. You turned around and invested in the auto industry, and I believe saved millions of jobs. And yet for some reason, the public just doesn't get it. Because you're losing the war of sound bites. You're losing the media cycles.
<v Walt Rowen>您的经济刺激计划实际上资助了一个病入膏肓的金融体系，这对小企业主来说至关重要。您转而投资汽车行业，我相信这挽救了数百万个工作岗位。然而出于某种原因，公众就是不买账。因为您在精炼言论（sound bites）的战争中失利了。您在媒体舆论周期中处于下风。

00:39:48.560 --> 00:40:22.480
<v Ira Glass>On issue after issue, Republicans have catchy, understandable sound bites, and the Democrats seem to have nothing. The stimulus is another one where Democrats are fighting a well articulated, oft-repeated, Republican narrative about deficits and a failure to deliver results with, really, pretty much nothing. On health care, the lack of any persuasive message has left Democrats running from the issue. It's barely mentioned in TV ads in congressional races around the country. On finance reform, there's been no understandable explanation for what the hell led us into the recession and what is needed to fix it.
<v Ira Glass>在接连不断的问题上，共和党人拥有抓人眼球、通俗易懂的简短言论，而民主党人似乎什么也拿不出来。经济刺激计划是又一个例子：共和党人有一个表达清晰、反复吟唱的叙事逻辑，主张财政赤字和无法兑现成果，而民主党人几乎拿不出任何实质性的东西来迎战。在医疗改革方面，缺乏任何有说服力的信息让民主党人对该议题唯恐避之不及。在全国各地的国会竞选的电视广告中，它几乎被隐去不提。在金融改革方面，对于究竟是什么鬼东西把我们引入了经济衰退以及需要什么来修复它，没有任何人给出过通俗易懂的解释。

00:40:22.480 --> 00:40:35.490
<v Ira Glass>Back in 1930s, FDR used high-profile hearings, Pecora hearings, to create a national consensus about what had gone wrong in the economy and a mandate for what to do next. Nothing has played that role for the Democrats today.
<v Ira Glass>早在20世纪30年代，罗斯福总统就利用高调的听证会——佩科拉听证会——在经济到底出了什么问题这一问题上达成了一共识，并为接下来该做的事赋予了合法授权。如今，没有任何东西为民主党人发挥过这样的作用。

00:40:35.490 --> 00:40:47.780
<v Ira Glass>Jack Hitt, who voted for Obama back in 2008, normally prefers to watch politics on the sidelines. But after witnessing this two-year display of lame-assery, he decided he had to go to Washington to try to understand.
<v Ira Glass>杰克·希特在2008年曾投票给奥巴马，他通常更喜欢作壁上观来看政治。但在目睹了这长达两年的无能表现（lame-assery）之后，他决定必须亲自去一趟华盛顿，试图弄明白这一切。

00:40:47.780 --> 00:41:18.560
<v Jack Hitt>What happened? These new Democrats who came to power in 2008 were supposed to be such savvy communicators. Two years ago, all over the country, Democrats adored Obama's verbal jujitsu, his team's rapid response to attacks and daily messages. When Sarah Palin tried the "palling around with terrorists" shtick, linking Obama to '60s radical Bill Ayers, the rapid response team laughed it off, saying, "Sure, Obama denounced these terrible bombings that happened. When he was eight years old." They knew how to do it.
<v Jack Hitt>究竟发生了什么？这些在2008年掌权的新民主党人本该是极其精明的沟通高手。两年前，在全国各地，民主党人都崇拜奥巴马高超的言语交锋技巧、他的团队对攻击的迅速反应以及每日发布的信息。当莎拉·帕林试图玩弄“与恐怖分子交朋友”的伎俩，把奥巴马和60年代激进分子比尔·艾尔斯联系在一起时，快速反应团队对此付诸一笑，并说：“当然，奥巴马谴责了这些可怕的爆炸事件。当他八岁的时候。”他们分明知道该怎么做。

00:41:18.560 --> 00:41:39.030
<v Jack Hitt>The campaign slogan, "Yes we can," seemed as much about saying that Democrats could create a message machine just as effective as Fox News, the right wing thing tanks, and the weekend echo chamber of deployed pundits. Where did that aggressiveness go? When Republicans called the President a Muslim, Barack Obama himself said, "I will always put my money--"
<v Jack Hitt>竞选口号“是的，我们能”，似乎同样在表达民主党人可以建立一个与福克斯新闻、右翼智库以及周末轮番上阵的专家回音室同样高效的信息机器。那种好斗精神去哪儿了？当共和党人称总统为穆斯林时，贝拉克·奥巴马本人却说：“我将永远把我的赌注压在……”

00:41:39.030 --> 00:41:49.000
<v Barack Obama>On the American people. And I'm not going to be worrying too much about whatever rumors are floating around.
<v Barack Obama>美国人民身上。而且我不会过分担心任何正在流传的谣言。

00:41:49.000 --> 00:42:10.400
<v Jack Hitt>Really? I mean, by now even my mother knows that if you don't respond to an attack, then the attack becomes the new reality. If your enemies insist that you are a Bolshevik and no one responds with a coherent counter message, then people who think you are a Bolshevik will get elected to Congress in disturbing numbers. Which is about to happen.
<v Jack Hitt>真的吗？我是说，到现在为止，连我母亲都知道，如果你不对攻击进行回击，那么攻击就会变成新的现实。如果你的敌人坚称你是布尔什维克，而没有人用连贯的反击信息予以回应，那么那些认为你是布尔什维克的人就会以令人不安的数量当选国会议员。而这正要发生了。

00:42:10.400 --> 00:42:29.830
<v Jack Hitt>Is no one who could call them up calling them up and telling them the obvious? Or maybe there's some theory behind the inaction that we're just not seeing. Maybe this is one of Obama's long game tactics, one of his legendary triple bank shots. For nearly two years I've wondered, what's going on over there? What are the Democratic strategists thinking?
<v Jack Hitt>难道就没有人能给他们打电话，把这些显而易见的事情告诉他们吗？或者，这种不作为背后其实有什么我们看不到的深层策略？也许这是奥巴马的长远战术之一，是他那些传奇的“三次库克撞球”（triple bank shots）中的一招？近两年来我一直在纳闷，那边到底在搞什么鬼？民主党战略家们到底在想些什么？

00:42:29.830 --> 00:42:48.060
<v Jack Hitt>To find out, I turn to one of the top insiders. Someone who's close to Rahm Emanuel, and talks to David Axelrod. Who meets with the top Democrats in the House and Senate. Who writes an occasional candidate speech and also works the media side as a commentator on CNN. The former Clinton aide, Paul Begala.
<v Jack Hitt>为了找出答案，我求助于一位核心内幕人士。一个与拉姆·伊曼纽尔关系密切、常与大卫·阿克塞尔罗德交谈的人。一个会见众议院和参议院顶级民主党人的人。一个偶尔撰写候选人演讲稿、同时也在CNN担任评论员玩转媒体的人。前克林顿助手，保罗·贝加拉。

00:42:48.060 --> 00:43:02.680
<v Jack Hitt>The first thing I learned when we sat down in a conference room in DC was not reassuring. He's easily as frustrated with the Democrats as I am. Take the recent statement from the President in The New York Times that, quote, "bipartisan cooperation will flower right after the election."
<v Jack Hitt>我在华盛顿的一间会议室里坐下时学到的第一件事，就一点也让人放心不下来。他对民主党的失望程度一点也不比我低。拿总统最近在《纽约时报》上的声明来说，原话是，“两党合作将在大选结束后马上开花结果”。

00:43:02.680 --> 00:43:05.410
<v Paul Begala>When monkeys fly out my ass.
<v Paul Begala>除非母猪上树。

00:43:05.410 --> 00:43:35.840
<v Jack Hitt>Anyone who's watched the last 15 years knows how smartly Republicans craft their messages. Grover Norquist or some other leading conservative strategist faxes or emails out the topic of the day and a list of phrases to use in discussing it. Hence the term "talking points memo." Conservatives everywhere fan out and repeat the talking points in every form of media. Everybody's talking about it, so their position seems inevitable. If the Democrats are attempting anything like this, it's some very subtle jujitsu.
<v Jack Hitt>任何看过过去15年政治局势的人都知道，共和党人策划宣传攻势有多精明。格罗ver·诺奎斯特或其他某个领先的保守派战略家会发传真或电子邮件，公布当天的热议话题以及在讨论时应使用的短语列表。因此就有了“口径备忘录”这个词。各地的保守派蜂拥而出，在各种媒体上重复这些口径。每个人都在谈论它，所以他们的立场看起来就成了不可避免的选择。如果民主党人也试图搞这种把戏，那也就是某种非常微妙的柔术。

00:43:35.840 --> 00:43:41.850
<v Jack Hitt>I put it to Begala, is what looks to me like a lack of strategy, actually a strategy?
<v Jack Hitt>我向贝加拉提出，在我看来缺乏战略的做法，实际上是一种战略吗？

00:43:41.850 --> 00:43:56.190
<v Jack Hitt>For example, during the health care debate, right, the other side starts shouting "death panels." And the Obama administration and the Democratic party at large has no answer to this, right? They don't have a theme of the day that they're promoting for health care reform.
<v Jack Hitt>例如，在医疗改革辩论期间，对吧，另一方开始大喊“死亡小组”。而奥巴马政府和整个民主党对此毫无回应，对吧？他们没有在推行任何关于医改的主题。

00:43:56.190 --> 00:44:20.790
<v Paul Begala>You know, the answer is, you want to see a death panel? I will take you to a death panel. And go to a big private insurance company, stand out in front of their building, and tell the story of the girl-- I can't remember her name now, I think it was Natalie-- there was a little girl who was denied her care and she died. That's a real death panel. And it happens every day in the private insurance system. That's the real answer.
<v Paul Begala>你知道，答案应该是，你想看死亡小组吗？我带你去看看死亡小组。然后去一家大型私营保险公司，站在他们大楼外面，讲讲那个女孩的故事——我现在记不清她的名字了，好像叫娜塔莉——有个小女孩被拒绝提供治疗，然后她死了。那才是真正的死亡小组。这种事在私营保险系统中每天都在发生。这才是真正的答案。

00:44:20.790 --> 00:44:25.400
<v Jack Hitt>That is the real answer. So why didn't Democrats hear that two years ago?
<v Jack Hitt>这才是真正的答案。那为什么民主党人两年前没有听到这个？

00:44:25.400 --> 00:44:27.180
<v Paul Begala>Why didn't they say it?
<v Paul Begala>他们为什么不说？

00:44:27.180 --> 00:44:27.680
<v Jack Hitt>Why didn't they say it?
<v Jack Hitt>他们为什么不说？

00:44:27.680 --> 00:44:30.230
<v Paul Begala>I called everybody I know. I did.
<v Paul Begala>我给我认识的所有人都打了电话。真的打了。

00:44:30.230 --> 00:44:31.420
<v Jack Hitt>And what did they say?
<v Jack Hitt>他们怎么说？

00:44:31.420 --> 00:44:49.550
<v Paul Begala>You know, some said, "Sure, fine," and then they went of and did something else. Others-- there's a real fear in the Democratic party of appearing anti-business. And Democrats are not anti-business, but they're really worried about it. There's a terrible concern they're going to appear anti-business. They worry endlessly.
<v Paul Begala>你知道，有些人说，“行吧，好吧”，然后他们就去忙别的了。还有一些人——民主党内部有一种真正的恐惧，害怕显得反商。民主党人并不是反商，但他们真的很担心这一点。他们极其担心自己会被认为反商。他们无休止地焦虑。

00:44:49.550 --> 00:45:24.030
<v Jack Hitt>Or take the "Obama is a Muslim" line. Rush Limbaugh now regularly refers to the President "Imam Obama." If this were say, Joy Behar, referring to the Republicans as Klan members-- actually that happened. And the Republican response was one you've seen dozens of times on all sorts of issues. The pundits fanned out onto the talk shows, turned indignantly to whichever unlucky Democrat happened to be booked on the panel, and demanded, "Tom, I would love to discuss the economy. But before that, I insist that you join me in denouncing Joy Behar for comparing Republicans to the Klan."
<v Jack Hitt>或者拿“奥巴马是穆斯林”这种言论来说。拉什·林堡现在经常把总统称为“伊玛目奥巴马”。如果换作是，比方说，乔伊·贝哈尔，把共和党人称为三K党成员——其实这事发生过。而共和党人的反应则是你在各种问题上见过几十次的那种。专家们涌入脱口秀节目，义愤填膺地转向任何恰好被安排上节目的倒霉民主党人，要求道：“汤姆，我很想讨论经济问题。但在那之前，我坚持要求你和我一起谴责乔伊·贝哈尔将共和党人比作三K党。”

00:45:24.030 --> 00:45:43.050
<v Jack Hitt>That's how it's done. Push back against ridiculous language. Put the other side on the defensive. Make them cringe. A standard part of the Republican play book for the last 20 years. Why don't Democrats just do that? Well, Begala actually knows the answer. He says Barack Obama doesn't want to offend anybody.
<v Jack Hitt>事情就是这么办的。回击荒谬的言论。把对方逼入守势。让他们感到难堪。这是过去20年里共和党政治手册中的标准套路。为什么民主党人就是不这么做？嗯，贝加拉其实知道答案。他说巴拉克·奥巴马不想冒犯任何人。

00:45:43.050 --> 00:45:57.820
<v Paul Begala>There's a billion Muslims in this world, and there's nothing wrong with them. And there's a whole lot of Muslims in American, and whole lot in his military who are risking their lives for us. So he can't accept any kind of rubric that suggests that somehow it's a pejorative to call you a Muslim. I'm just saying for the President himself. Now--
<v Paul Begala>这个世界上有十亿穆斯林，他们没有什么不对的。美国有大量的穆斯林，他的军队里也有大量穆斯林正在为我们冒着生命危险。所以他不能接受任何暗示称叫你穆斯林是一种贬义的说法。我只是在说总统本人。现在——

00:45:57.820 --> 00:45:59.800
<v Jack Hitt>He does have a party surrounding him.
<v Jack Hitt>他身边确实有一个政党。

00:45:59.800 --> 00:46:00.730
<v Paul Begala>Exactly.
<v Paul Begala>没错。

00:46:00.730 --> 00:46:01.350
<v Jack Hitt>What's going on?
<v Jack Hitt>这到底是怎么回事？

00:46:01.350 --> 00:46:06.330
<v Paul Begala>Exactly. That's where he needs his side, and his party, to put it out.
<v Paul Begala>没错。这就是他需要他的阵营和他的政党把这些声音发出来的地方。

00:46:06.330 --> 00:46:16.790
<v Jack Hitt>In Kentucky, Jack Conway has asserted that Rand Paul, a million years ago, did something that was vaguely non-Christian, and all the Republicans have their petticoats up. OK?
<v Jack Hitt>在肯塔基州，杰克·康威断言兰德·保罗在很久以前做过一件隐晦的非基督教行为，所有的共和党人都像炸了毛一样反应过激。对吧？

00:46:16.790 --> 00:46:37.430
<v Ira Glass>Let me just explain what I'm talking about here. Jack Conway is the Democrat running for Senate in Kentucky, and he ran a TV ad about his opponent, Republican Rand Paul, about how when he was a student at a Christian college, Rand Paul was a member of a secret society that mocked Christianity. Conway is being attacked from all sides for questioning his opponent's faith on TV.
<v Ira Glass>我来解释一下我在说什么。杰克·康威是竞选肯塔基州参议员的民主党人，他播放了一则关于其对手、共和党人兰德·保罗的电视广告，内容是兰德·保罗在基督教大学当学生时，曾是一个嘲笑基督教的秘密社团的成员。康威因为在电视上质疑对手的信仰而遭到各方攻击。

00:46:37.430 --> 00:46:42.980
<v Jack Hitt>And Conway may lose the election because he's brought-- because of this issue.
<v Jack Hitt>康威可能会因为提出这个议题而输掉选举。

00:46:42.980 --> 00:46:49.920
<v Paul Begala>When they do show some cojones, there's always someone saying, "Oh, don't do that, don't do that." And I think that-- [INTERPOSING VOICES]
<v Paul Begala>当他们真的表现出一点魄力时，总会有人说，“哦，别那样做，别那样做。”我认为——[声音重叠]

00:46:49.920 --> 00:46:50.840
<v Jack Hitt>I've got to jump in on this.
<v Jack Hitt>我得插话谈谈这个。

00:46:50.840 --> 00:46:51.340
<v Paul Begala>OK.
<v Paul Begala>好的。

00:46:51.000 --> 00:46:58.530
<v Jack Hitt>You're right, Conway had some cojones. But who are the people who jumped on him for running that ad? Democrats.
<v Jack Hitt>你说得对，康威确实有点魄力。但那些因为他播放那则广告而对进行攻击的人是谁？是民主党人。

00:46:58.530 --> 00:47:01.730
<v Paul Begala>Some did. I defended it. I did. Strongly.
<v Paul Begala>有些人是这样。我为它进行了辩护。真的辩护了。态度很强烈。

00:47:01.730 --> 00:47:22.590
<v Jack Hitt>No. It just-- it seems like there's always an excuse for why Democrats have to retreat and cringe. You know, there's always, like, well we don't want to offend the Muslims, so we're not going to engage on the Obama is a Muslim President. We don't want to engage on death panels or health care too much, because it might make us look anti-insurance company or anti-business. So there's always an excuse for why they can't push back.
<v Jack Hitt>不。这只是——感觉总是有借口解释为什么民主党人必须退缩和畏缩。你知道，总会是这样，比如我们不想冒犯穆斯林，所以我们不打算介入“奥巴马是穆斯林总统”这个话题。我们不想过多介入死亡小组或医疗改革，因为这可能会让我们显得反保险公司或反商业。所以对于为什么他们无法进行回击，总能找到借口。

00:47:22.590 --> 00:47:23.550
<v Paul Begala>That's right.
<v Paul Begala>没错。

00:47:23.550 --> 00:47:52.740
<v Jack Hitt>Then, in early September, Obama and his team seemed to wake up. Obama said that the Republicans were holding, quote, "Middle-class tax cuts hostage to millionaire and billionaire cuts." The next day, Treasury Secretary Tim Geithner repeated that line almost word for word. A real talking point. I looked forward to one of those Jon Stewart bits where he quotes a half dozen officials robotically reciting the same sound bite before he rests his face in his open palms. Only this time it would be Democrats, not Republicans.
<v Jack Hitt>然后，在9月初，奥巴马和他的团队似乎醒悟过来了。奥巴马表示，共和党人正在把“中产阶级减税作为人质，来换取给百万和亿万富翁的减税”。第二天，财政部长蒂姆·盖特纳几乎一字不差地重复了这句话。一个真正的口径。我当时很期待乔恩·斯图尔特做那种讽刺节目，他在双手掩面之前，会引用半打官员像机器人一样背诵同一个精彩片段。只不过这一次主角会是民主党人，而不是共和党人。

00:47:52.740 --> 00:48:01.990
<v Barack Obama>They want to hold these middle-class tax cuts hostage until they get an additional tax cut for the wealthiest 2% of Americans.
<v Barack Obama>他们想把这些中产阶级减税当作人质，直到他们为美国最富有的2%的人争取到额外的减税。

00:48:01.990 --> 00:48:08.990
<v Tim Geithner>Hold middle-class tax cuts hostage to make permanent tax cuts that go to just the top 2% of earners in the country.
<v Tim Geithner>把中产阶级减税当成人质，以换取让仅属于该国收入最高2%的人群的减税政策永久化。

00:48:08.990 --> 00:48:16.710
<v Barack Obama>$700 billion to give a tax cut worth an average of $100,000 to millionaires and billionaires.
<v Barack Obama>7000亿美元用来给百万和亿万富翁提供平均价值10万美元的减税。

00:48:16.710 --> 00:48:20.090
<v Tim Geithner>To have us go out and borrow $700 billion from our children.
<v Tim Geithner>让我们去向我们的子孙后代借这7000亿美元。

00:48:20.090 --> 00:48:26.120
<v Barack Obama>And it's a tax cut economists say would do little to add momentum to our economy.
<v Barack Obama>而经济学家表示，这是一项对为我们的经济增添动能无济于事的减税政策。

00:48:26.120 --> 00:48:31.340
<v Tim Geithner>Little impact on job creation and years of stagnation in middle-class wages.
<v Tim Geithner>对创造就业机会影响甚微，并导致中产阶级工资多年停滞。

00:48:31.340 --> 00:48:44.520
<v Jack Hitt>I thought, OK, this election is on. The Democrats would force the issue to the floor and make Republicans vote against a middle-class tax cut weeks before an election. We do have a two party system, and the system is working.
<v Jack Hitt>我想，好吧，这场选举要动真格的了。民主党人将把这个议题提交到议会前台，迫使共和党人在选举前几周投票反对中产阶级减税。我们确实是一个两党制国家，而且这个系统正在运作。不出所料……

00:48:44.520 --> 00:49:08.040
<v Jack Hitt>Predictably, the Republicans pushed back with classic rapid response. Senator John Kyl distilled this into two words: class warfare. The Democrats' response? They panicked, and went into their usual crouch. A day later it was decided, the issue would be shelved until after the election. They would not run on tax cuts, the most effective political issue in America.
<v Jack Hitt>不出所料，共和党人以经典的迅速反应进行了反击。参议员约翰·基尔将这精简为两个词：阶级斗争。民主党人的反应呢？他们惊慌失措，又回到了他们惯常的畏缩状态。一天后决定，这个议题将被搁置到大选之后。他们不会以减税为竞选纲领，而这本来是美国最有效的政治议题。

00:49:08.040 --> 00:49:09.880
<v Jack Hitt>They shelved it. Explain that to me.
<v Jack Hitt>他们把它搁置了。请向我解释一下这是为什么。

00:49:09.880 --> 00:49:27.570
<v Paul Begala>I can't. I can't defend it, put it that way. Explain it, because they let the senators who are up in this election cycle make the decision on whether they voted on it. And the ones who were up in the cycle said, "You know, I'd just as soon just go home and run my campaign, rather than have the tax issue up." I think it was a colossal mistake.
<v Paul Begala>我解释不了。我无法为之辩护，这么说吧。要解释的话，是因为他们让在这个选举周期面临连任的参议员们来决定是否对它进行投票。那些面临连任的人说：“你知道的，我宁愿直接回家去搞我的竞选，也不愿把税收议题摆在台面上。”我认为这是一个巨大的错误。

00:49:27.570 --> 00:49:42.250
<v Paul Begala>I cannot imagine the Republicans leaving an issue on the table that they win by two to one, eight weeks before an election. I cannot imagine that. It is a divisive issue, by the way, but it divides two to one for the Democrats. It really infuriated me.
<v Paul Begala>我无法想象共和党人会在距离大选还有八周的时候，把一个支持率二比一稳操胜券的议题晾在一边。我无法想象这一点。顺便说一句，这是一个具有分裂性的议题，但对民主党而言，支持它的比例是二比一。这真的把我气疯了。

00:49:42.250 --> 00:50:02.520
<v Paul Begala>But, see, that's what I mean. There are people in my party-- and, believe me, I was involved in meetings with strategists, not politicians, where we were screaming and I was hurling foul language about this. And the view was, Democrats can never win a race on taxes. We can never win if the issue is taxes.
<v Paul Begala>但是，你看，这正是我要说的。我党内有人——相信我，我曾参加过战略家而非政客的会议，我们在会上大喊大叫，我甚至为了这事爆了粗口。而当时的看法是，民主党人在税收问题上永远赢不了竞选。如果议题是税收，我们永远无法获胜。

00:50:02.520 --> 00:50:26.990
<v Paul Begala>I just don't agree. I went to them. I gave them a chart out of The Wall Street Journal that tracked the tax issue during the '08 election. On Labor Day, John McCain and Barack Obama were tied on the question of who do you trust on the issue of taxes. Barack Obama went out every single day after that and said, "If you make less than $250,000, I'm going to cut your taxes. If you make more, I'm not." Every day. By election day, he was ahead, I think, by 16 points. Maybe 14.
<v Paul Begala>我就是不同意。我去找了他们。我给他们看了一张来自《华尔街日报》的图表，上面追踪了08年大选期间的税收议题。劳动节那天，约翰·麦凯恩和巴拉克·奥巴马在“你信任谁来处理税收问题”这一问题上打成平手。在那之后的每一天，巴拉克·奥巴马都出去说：“如果你收入低于25万美元，我要给你减税。如果你收入更高，我就不减。”每一天都在说。到了选举日，他领先了，我想，有16个百分点。也许是14个。

00:50:26.990 --> 00:50:33.420
<v Paul Begala>So what does that tell you? It tells you, if you wage the fight, you can win the fight. But you can't win it if you don't fight.
<v Paul Begala>这说明了什么？这说明，如果你发起抗争，你就能赢得这场抗争。但如果你不抗争，你就赢不了。

00:50:33.420 --> 00:50:52.180
<v Jack Hitt>I'm going to back to this question. So why-- do you have any inside information as to why the Obama administration and the Democratic party at large abandoned a sure thing for this campaign, tactically? Was there a secret theory that they had that this would work in their favor?
<v Jack Hitt>我要回到这个问题上来。所以为什么——你有没有什么内部消息，解释为什么奥巴马政府和整个民主党在战术上放弃了一个十拿九稳的竞选议题？他们是不是有什么秘密理论，觉得这样做对他们有利？

00:50:52.180 --> 00:50:56.860
<v Paul Begala>They believed that any discussion of taxes would be to their detriment.
<v Paul Begala>他们相信任何对税收的讨论都会对他们不利。

00:50:56.860 --> 00:51:16.980
<v Jack Hitt>There's always an excuse. I had come to Washington to understand the new media strategy of the revived Democratic party. Instead, I discovered something the dated back almost a century, to when Will Rogers first cracked that joke. "I'm not a member of an organized party. I'm a Democrat." I couldn't believe it. Again, I turned to Begala.
<v Jack Hitt>总是有借口。我来到华盛顿是为了解复兴后的民主党的新媒体战略。相反，我发现了一些可以追溯到近一个世纪前的东西，也就是威尔·罗杰斯第一次开那个玩笑的时候。“我不是任何有组织政党的成员。我是一名民主党人。”我简直不敢相信。我再次转向贝加拉。

00:51:16.980 --> 00:51:28.080
<v Jack Hitt>It's not even clear that the Democrats have a messaging machine. Is there a phone call? Does a fax go out? Is an email-- is anybody in touch with anybody every day? Is there?
<v Jack Hitt>甚至连民主党是否拥有一个宣传机器都还不清楚。会有电话通知吗？会发出传真吗？会有电子邮件——每天有人跟任何人保持联系吗？有吗？

00:51:28.080 --> 00:51:46.430
<v Paul Begala>My guess is it's those three different operations, the House, the Senate, and the White House. And they try to intersect as much as they can. But, no, I don't think the Democrats have this sort of coordination. It's looser. I've been working toward that goal all my career. But I don't think we're there. But it is true, they're never going to be as organized. Now, there is--
<v Paul Begala>我猜是那三个不同的运作机构，众议院、参议院和白宫。他们会尽其所能地产生交集。但是，没有，我不认为民主党有这种程度的协调。它比较松散。我职业生涯一直在朝着那个目标努力。但我认为我们还没到那个地步。不过确实，他们永远不会像对方那么有组织。现在，有——

00:51:46.430 --> 00:51:47.130
<v Jack Hitt>But why is that?
<v Jack Hitt>但这是为什么呢？

00:51:47.130 --> 00:52:04.040
<v Paul Begala>It's the nature of the culture. It just is. I'm serious. I mean, I've done center-left parties all around the world. And in the main, people who tend to be very corporate, very hierarchical, very obedient, tend to be conservative. We don't just sit waiting for orders on the left.
<v Paul Begala>这是文化的天性。就是这样。我是认真的。我是说，我研究过世界各地许多中左翼政党。总的来说，那些往往非常注重企业化、非常讲究等级制度、非常听话的人，往往倾向于保守派。我们在左翼可不会只是坐在那里等候命令。

00:52:04.040 --> 00:52:17.230
<v Jack Hitt>So there is no daily phone call or email that goes out from the White House, saying here's what we want to push today. Here's what we want to try to-- Here's what you should be talking about, or alerting the nation to.
<v Jack Hitt>所以白宫没有每天打出的电话或发出的电子邮件，说这是我们今天要力推的内容。这是我们想要尝试的——这是你应该谈论的，或者是提醒全国注意的。

00:52:17.230 --> 00:52:28.700
<v Paul Begala>Right. No, there is definitely is an email that goes out every day to talkers. And there are talkers' calls and so forth. I have to say, I don't get on them. And I'm a pretty loyal Democrat. And I don't read their talking points on the air. I don't.
<v Paul Begala>对。不，每天确实会有一封发给发言人的电子邮件，讨论各种话题。也有发言人的电话会议等等。我必须说，我并不参加这些电话会议。而且我是一个相当忠诚的民主党人。我不在节目中宣读他们的口径。我才不呢。

00:52:28.700 --> 00:52:30.550
<v Jack Hitt>Maybe that explains what's happening.
<v Jack Hitt>也许这就解释了正在发生的事情。

00:52:30.550 --> 00:52:40.980
<v Paul Begala>That's what I mean, it's a cultural thing. Here am I, a total Democrat, and I'm an Obama Democrat, and I get stuff from the White House and I delete it. I was like, I"m not going to read that crap on the air. I think for myself.
<v Paul Begala>这正是我所说的，这是一种文化现象。我明明是一个百分之百的民主党人，而且我是支持奥巴马的民主党人，我收到白宫发来的东西却直接把它删了。我想的是，我才不在节目里念那些垃圾。我有独立思考能力。

00:52:40.980 --> 00:52:54.760
<v Jack Hitt>Paul, you were just saying how you were trying to build this infrastructure to mimic the Grover Norquist machine. I mean, if you're not paying attention to your own machine, or the one that you're trying to build, why would any Democrat?
<v Jack Hitt>保罗，你刚才还在说你如何试图建立这种基础设施来模仿格罗ver·诺奎斯特的机器。我的意思是，如果你自己都不关注你自己的机器，或者你正试图建立的那台机器，那为什么任何民主党人要关注？

00:52:54.760 --> 00:53:07.290
<v Paul Begala>Right. That's a good point. But, also, some of it is, I think, as I said, we like to think for ourselves. Second, you know, you're going to have to bleep this, but my [BLEEP] is better than their [BLEEP], so I don't need their [BLEEP].
<v Paul Begala>对。这倒是个好点子。但是，同时，其中一部分原因，我认为正如我所说的那样，我们喜欢独立思考。其次，你知道的，你到时候得把这个消音，但我的[哔——]比他们的[哔——]要好，所以我不需要他们的[哔——]。

00:53:07.290 --> 00:53:28.200
<v Jack Hitt>Did you get that? Even Begala, who's dream has been to create such a machine, admits that he'd rather hit the delete button and fling is own poo. Which explains why the many voices of the Democrats today sound like argle bargle white noise, muttered into a thousand microphones, against which the clarity of the conservative position rings as the only truth anyone can hear.
<v Jack Hitt>你听到了吗？甚至连梦想就是创建这样一台机器的贝加拉也承认，他宁愿按删除键去扔自己的大便。这就解释了为什么今天民主党人的诸多声音听起来就像是一片胡言乱语的白噪音，对着上千个话筒喃喃自语，而在这衬托下，保守派立场的清晰度却成了任何人都能听到的唯一真理。

00:53:28.200 --> 00:53:36.940
<v Jack Hitt>Though Begala told me he actually thinks the Democrats have gotten better in the last few weeks, and that there will be some surprises among the massive losses. Constructive surprises.
<v Jack Hitt>尽管贝加拉告诉我，他其实认为民主党人在过去几周里有所改善，并且在惨重失败中会出现一些惊喜。建设性的惊喜。

00:53:36.940 --> 00:53:47.190
<v Paul Begala>You will find the Democrats who survived are the ones who had some spine. They're not the ones who ran away saying, "Oh, I didn't vote for health care and I didn't--" you know? No. They're going to be the ones who stood up.
<v Paul Begala>你会发现那些幸存下来的民主党人都是有点骨气的人。他们不是那些逃跑并喊着“哦，我没有投票赞成医疗改革，我没有——”你知道的？不。他们将是那些挺身而出的人。

00:53:47.190 --> 00:54:18.630
<v Paul Begala>I'm leaving now, tonight, literally, to go to Ohio to campaign for this guy John Boccieri. Iraq war veteran, he represents a district that had been Republican for 60 years before he was elected. He cast one of the deciding votes for the health care bill. And he was asked about it in his heavily Republican district. And he said, "You know, I used to think I was brave and I used to think I was tough. And I've flown into enemy fire, been on four combat missions. But I'm just not tough enough to look this lady in the eye who's got breast cancer and tell her I'm going to deny her health care. I'm not tough enough to do that. If you want somebody that tough, you've got to vote for a Republican."
<v Paul Begala>我马上就要动身了，就在今晚，字面意义上，去俄亥俄州为这个叫约翰·博恰里的家伙助选。伊拉克战争退伍军人，在他当选之前，他代表的一个选区连续60年都是共和党人的天下。他对医改法案投下了决定性的一票。在他那个共和党势力强大的选区里，有人问起这件事。他说：“你知道的，我过去认为自己很勇敢，我过去认为自己很顽强。我曾飞入敌人的火力网，执行过四次战斗任务。但我就是不够顽强，无法直视这位患有乳腺癌的女士的眼睛，告诉她我要剥夺她的医疗保障。我没有那么顽强去这样做。如果你想要一个那么顽强的人，那你得去投共和党人的票。”

00:54:18.630 --> 00:54:36.840
<v Paul Begala>I love this guy. I think John Boccieri is going to win. I hope he does, I'm going out to help him. But if he does, people will say, well maybe I ought to be more like that than like some of these, you know, wimpier Democrats.
<v Paul Begala>我太喜欢这家伙了。我认为约翰·博恰里会赢。我希望他能赢，我要去帮他。但如果他赢了，人们就会说，也许我应该更像那样的人，而不是像其中某些你知道的更懦弱的民主党人。

00:54:36.840 --> 00:55:24.740
<v Jack Hitt>Everyone is comparing this year's coming tsunami to the wave election of 1994. That's when the Republicans took control of the House from the Democrats for the first time since 1947. But that was only half the story. The Republicans in those days were led by a lethargic bore of a man named Bob Michael. He was rotund in a 19th century cartoon sort of way and spoke in tones that would silence a colicky baby. The young punk and back-bencher Newt Gingrich had declared war that year, not only on Democrats, but on Bob Michael. Michael's certainty that Democrats would always control the House and that his job was to speak carefully and collect their crumbs was the real corruption Gingrich identified as the rot in the old Republican party. One that would welcome conservative ideas.
<v Jack Hitt>每个人都在把今年即将到来的海啸与1994年的浪潮大选相提并论。那是共和党自1947年以来首次从民主党手中夺取众议院控制权的时候。但那只是故事的一半。当时共和党人的领袖是一个名叫鲍勃·迈克尔的沉闷乏味的人。他胖得像19世纪漫画里的人物，说话的语气能让哭闹的婴儿安静下来。年轻的毛头小子兼后排议员纽特·金里奇在那一年宣战，不仅对民主党，也对鲍勃·迈克尔宣战。迈克尔坚信民主党将永远控制众议院，而他的工作就是谨言慎行并捡拾他们的残羹冷炙，金里奇认为这正是老共和党内部腐烂的真正根源。一个会欢迎保守派思想的根源。

00:55:24.740 --> 00:56:02.350
<v Jack Hitt>The current Tea Party rebellion is much the same. They've primaried and overthrown some of the most establishment old bull conservatives in Congress. And next year they'll probably destroy Republican moderates, like Lindsey Graham of South Carolina and Olympia Snow of Maine. Meanwhile, Democrats live one election at a time, one issue at a time, unaware that they've become electoral wallpaper, in front of which daring conservatives delight in performing, and that the Democrats' only hope is to understand that their real enemy is not the Republican party, but their own imaginations.
<v Jack Hitt>当前的茶党叛乱也非常相似。他们在国会中通过初选推翻了一些最具建制派色彩的老牌保守派大佬。明年他们很可能会摧毁共和党温和派，比如南卡罗来纳州的林赛·格雷厄姆和缅因州的奥林匹亚·斯诺。与此同时，民主党人过着走一步算一步的日子，一个问题接一个问题地应对，却没有意识到自己已经成了选举中的背景墙，大胆的保守派们乐于在这面墙前尽情表演，而民主党唯一的希望是明白他们真正的敌人不是共和党，而是他们自己的想象力。

00:56:02.350 --> 00:56:05.170
<v Ira Glass>Jack Hitt lives in New Haven.
<v Ira Glass>杰克·希特住在纽黑文。

00:56:05.170 --> 00:56:52.040
